e first paragraph of t","Sunday, June 28, 2026, 19:50  +0530",https://zeenews.india.com/world/treachery-iran-condemns-american-terrorist-army-for-attacks-on-surveillance-facilitiestreachery-iran-condemns-american-terrorist-army-for-attacks-on-surveillance-facilities-3058209.html,2026-06-29 02:11:32
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"US-Iran war LIVE updates: Tehran, Washington agree to halt attacks, will hold talks in Qatar","US-Iran war LIVE updates: Tehran, Washington agree to halt attacks, will hold talks in Qatar","US-Iran war LIVE updates: Tehran, Washington agree to halt attacks, will hold talks in Qatar US-Iran war LIVE updates: Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps on Sunday said it launched missiles and drones at the Ali Al Salem Air Base in Kuwait and the 5th Fleet naval base in Salman Port, Bahrain. US-Iran war LIVE updates:The United States and Iran have agreed to halt attacks after exchanging strikes over the weekend and have decided to meet in Qatar this week to discuss the crucial trade waterway, the Strait of Hormuz, and other issues to potentially end the war, Axios reported. A senior US official said the US-Iran memorandum of understanding (MoU) remains “on track.”...Read More ""Nothing has been cancelled. Technical talks regarding the implementation of the MoU are on track for the coming days as planned, and deconfliction channels are up and running after the Lake Lucerne Summit,"" the official said, referring to recent talks in Switzerland led on the US side by Vice President JD Vance, according to CNN.The current halt in tit-for-tat attacks comes after US President Donald Trump warned of further military action if attacks in the Strait of Hormuz continue. Meanwhile, Iran warned that any ceasefire violations would ""result in the complete halt of all diplomatic processes.""How did it start?The hostilities began last this week after Iran attacked the Singapore-flagged cargo vessel M/V Ever Lovely with a one-way attack drone on June 25 while it was exiting the Strait of Hormuz along the Omani coast. Trump called the attack a ""foolish"" violation of the ceasefire, and the US military said it struck Iranian missile and drone storage facilities in response over two days.Iran attacks Kuwait, BahrainIranian forces also responded on Sunday by attacking its Gulf neighbours Ku","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 07:07:23 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-iran-war-live-updates-peace-deal-strait-of-hormuz-strikes-qatar-donald-trump-araghchi-jd-vance-kuwait-bahrain-oil-101782695699377.html,2026-06-29 02:11:32
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Venezuela earthquake update: Two 11-year-old boys found alive days later; search for survivors continues,Venezuela earthquake update: Two 11-year-old boys found alive days later; search for survivors continues,"Venezuela earthquake update: Two 11-year-old boys found alive days later; search for survivors continues Two boys were rescued alive from Venezuela earthquake rubble as search teams continue working while families wait for missing relatives. Two 11-year-old boys were pulled alive from earthquake rubble in Venezuela over the weekend, giving families a rare moment of hope as rescue teams continue searching for survivors. Interim President Delcy Rodríguez said 33 people were rescued from collapsed buildings during the past two days. The rescues came several days after two powerful earthquakes struck the country within seconds of each other. Despite these successes, the situation remains grim. Officials say at least 1,450 people have died, while thousands of others are still unaccounted for. As families enter another night of waiting, rescue crews are racing against time to find anyone who may still be trapped beneath the debris. The magnitude 7.2 and 7.5 earthquakes struck Venezuela on Wednesday, only 39 seconds apart. Venezuela's interim President Delcy Rodríguez said nearly 800 buildings collapsed after the earthquakes, trapping many people under concrete and rubble. Rescue workers have continued searching through damaged neighborhoods even after the crucial first 72 hours, which experts say is usually the most important period for finding survivors. Officials believe some people may still be alive if they have access to food or water. Meanwhile, relatives have spent days searching for loved ones. In several areas, families have been digging through rubble themselves while waiting for heavy equipment to arrive. Also Read:Venezuela earthquake: Argentine footballer Lucas Trejo’s first response emerges after family t","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:56:30 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/venezuela-earthquake-update-two-11-year-old-boys-among-33-found-alive-days-later-as-search-continues-101782694539295.html,2026-06-29 02:11:32
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Electric Forest baby case: Michigan Police issue public appeal after newborn's body found at music festival,Electric Forest baby case: Michigan Police issue public appeal after newborn's body found at music festival,"Electric Forest baby case: Michigan Police issue public appeal after newborn's body found at music festival Electric Forest draws tens of thousands annually and works with state police to help manage security and safety during the festival.&nbsp; What was expected to be the closing day of Electric Forest 2026 instead turned into a scene of tragedy after a shocking discovery at the popular music festival. Authorities said the body of a newborn was found Sunday inside a portable toilet at the festival grounds in Rothbury, Michigan. According to The Detroit News, the discovery was made by an employee of a sanitation company while carrying out routine maintenance in the campground area. Multiple outlets later confirmed the incident. WOOD-TV reported that sources had verified the body of an infant was found at the festival site. It was also noted that Electric Forest, which attracts tens of thousands of attendees each year, contracts with state police to assist with security operations during the event. Authorities have not yet determined the exact age of the infant, and investigators continue to examine the circumstances surrounding the case. State police have appealed to the public for assistance as the investigation continues. In a statement posted on X, the West Michigan post urged anyone with potentially relevant information to contact authorities. Also read:Who was Daniel Riva? US diplomat fatally stabbed in Myanmar; 5 things to know as ex-wife under investigation ""If you were in the area and observed anything unusual, or if you have information you believe may be relevant, we encourage you to come forward,"" the statement said. Electric Forest organizers also addressed the incident in a message to attendees, expressi","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:49:01 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/electric-forest-baby-case-michigan-police-issue-public-appeal-after-newborns-body-found-at-music-festival-101782694374793.html,2026-06-29 02:11:32
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"After deadly earthquakes, floods devastate Venezuela; scary videos emerge","After deadly earthquakes, floods devastate Venezuela; scary videos emerge","After deadly earthquakes, floods devastate Venezuela; scary videos emerge Local media reported that the Chabasquencito River and several nearby streams burst their banks after more than eight hours of heavy rainfall. Days after twin earthquakes devastated large parts ofVenezuela, severe flooding has struck the municipality of Monseñor José Vicente de Unda in Portuguesa state. Hours of intense rainfall caused rivers and streams to overflow, inundating homes, businesses and public infrastructure. The number of affected residents remains unclear. The flooding comes as Venezuela continues rescue and recovery operations following the powerful earthquakes that struck on June 24. Emergency responders remain deployed across the country, where thousands of people are still missing, and many families remain displaced. Read more:Who was Yimvert Berroteran? Rising Venezuelan football star, 18, dies after earthquake strikes youth tournament Local mediareported that the Chabasquencito River and several nearby streams burst their banks after more than eight hours of heavy rainfall. Floodwaters swept through residential and commercial areas, carrying mud and debris into streets. Videos shared on social media showed muddy water rushing through streets and entering buildings and damaging structures. As fresh tremors shook nerves on Sunday, worries grew that the window for locating survivors was dwindling. Emergency responders battled to navigate chaotic highways congested by desperate civilian rescue operations in northern Venezuela, devastated by the earthquake. Delcy Rodriguez, the acting president of Venezuela, made broadcast comments on Sunday afternoon. She said that search-and-rescue operations were ","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:39:33 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/after-deadly-earthquakes-floods-devastate-venezuela-scary-videos-emerge-101782685643678.html,2026-06-29 02:11:32
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Shooting near Energizer Park, St. Louis prompts St. Louis City 2 vs Austin FC II game suspension: Reports","Shooting near Energizer Park, St. Louis prompts St. Louis City 2 vs Austin FC II game suspension: Reports","Shooting near Energizer Park, St. Louis prompts St. Louis City 2 vs Austin FC II game suspension: Reports A St. Louis City 2 vs. Austin FC II MLS NEXT Pro match at Energizer Park on Sunday was suspended due to a shooting incident nearby. Update: A shooting incident reported near Energizer Park in St. Louis on Sunday evening led to the suspension of the MLS NEXT Pro match between St. Louis CITY2 and Austin FC II. Initial reports indicate shots were fired at an apartment complex near North 17th and Chestnut streets at around 6:30 p.m. The location is approximately three blocks east of Energizer Park, according to KSDK. The match was halted as fans were asked to shelter in place. As of 7:25 p.m., no injuries had been reported. Play was stopped with the score tied 0-0. Authorities have not yet provided additional details about the incident. Also Read:Lansing shooting update: Reported shooting at E Malcolm X Street, Michigan; first details Initial report: A St. Louis City 2 vs. Austin FC II MLS NEXT Pro match at Energizer Park on Sunday was suspended due to ashootingincident nearby, according to STL Soccer News. Reports and social media updates from attendees said shots were fired in or around Energizer Park during the match. Fans sheltered in place and the match was halted. Also Read:Seth Scott Hatfield: Montreal shooting suspect identity and 104-page shocking manifesto revealed Witnesses described chaotic scenes inside the stadium, with some saying fans took cover on the concourse and near the seating areas. One witness reported, ""Pandemonium at the St Louis CITY2 match as someone outside the main gate started shooting towards the gate. We had arrived through that gate 2 minutes before this happened. I'm not sur","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 05:53:54 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/shooting-near-energizer-park-st-louis-prompts-st-louis-city-2-vs-austin-fc-ii-game-suspension-reports-101782692349198.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Twist in Candace Owens’ latest Erika Kirk criticism amid husband George Farmer's admission: ‘I don’t know…’,Twist in Candace Owens’ latest Erika Kirk criticism amid husband George Farmer's admission: ‘I don’t know…’,"Twist in Candace Owens’ latest Erika Kirk criticism amid husband George Farmer's admission: ‘I don’t know…’ Candace Owens’ comments about George Farmer sparked debate online, shifting attention from Erika Kirk criticism to her own marriage. Candace Owens is facing fresh criticism after a video from her show sparked debate online. In the clip, Owens questioned the close communication between late Turning Point USA founderCharlie Kirkand his wife,Erika Kirk. However, many viewers focused on a different part of her comments. While comparing their marriage to her own relationship with husband George Farmer, Owens said, “I don’t know what George does every day,” leading some critics to argue that she unintentionally revealed communication gaps in her own marriage. The discussion began when Owens spoke about reports that Charlie Kirk and Erika Kirk regularly discussed work, future plans and important decisions together. Reacting to that, Owens said, “I don’t know what George does every day. I’m in my basement all day.” She also expressed surprise at the idea of spouses discussing detailed plans and contingencies together, using her own marriage as a comparison. That comparison became the focus of online discussion that backfired on her. One viewer wrote, “She was trying to criticize Erika but ended up talking about her own marriage.” Another commented, “The part that stood out wasn’t Erika. It was Candace saying she has no idea what her husband does all day.” The clip has since been widely shared by both supporters and critics of Owens, with many debating whether her remarks revealed more about her own relationship than she intended. Also Read:Candace Owens questions authenticity of Charlie & Erika Kirk's marriage, li","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 05:29:02 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/twist-in-candace-owens-latest-erika-kirk-criticism-amid-husband-george-farmers-admission-i-don-t-know-101782688552237.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Castro Valley fire update: Smoke spreads to Hayward as Alameda County firefighters battle blaze,Castro Valley fire update: Smoke spreads to Hayward as Alameda County firefighters battle blaze,"Castro Valley fire update: Smoke spreads to Hayward as Alameda County firefighters battle blaze Dark gray smoke was seen rising east of Redwood Road in Castro Valley, reportedly near Castro Valley Boulevard. Dark gray smoke was seen rising east of Redwood Road in Castro Valley, reportedly near Castro Valley Boulevard, according to several posts circulating on social media. Fire crews are working to contain a major residential fire in Castro Valley that broke out at a home on Queen Street on Saturday afternoon. According to NBC Bay Area, the blaze was reported shortly before 4:15 p.m. Authorities said the fire posed a threat to neighboring properties, prompting officials to escalate the response and call a three-alarm fire as firefighters battled to prevent the flames from spreading. One Facebook user shared multiple photos appearing to show the scene and wrote, “Structure fire currently taking place on I believe Queen in Castro Valley. I'd avoid Grove. Smells like fireworks.” Another Facebook user later reported that crews from the Alameda County Fire Department had responded to a confirmed two-alarm structure fire on Queen Street. Also read:What happened in Mabank, Texas? Fire Chief Charlie Woodard fatally struck by vehicle after rodeo event The user also urged residents and motorists to steer clear of the area, writing, ""Avoid the area if possible."" People living nearby told the San Francisco Chronicle that a loud boom was heard moments before thick plumes of smoke began rising over the neighborhood. The incident appeared to cause major damage to at least one residence. Authorities at the scene said there were no known reports of injuries at the time. Ojas Jaiswal is a Content ","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 05:24:23 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/castro-valley-fire-update-smoke-spreads-to-hayward-as-alameda-county-firefighters-battle-blaze-101782690097936.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Why is Apple Maps not working? Users report directions unavailable as outage hits; 'everything is down',Why is Apple Maps not working? Users report directions unavailable as outage hits; 'everything is down',"Why is Apple Maps not working? Users report directions unavailable as outage hits; 'everything is down' According to Downdetector, reports of problems started around 7:10 p.m. EDT on Sunday. Update: Apple Maps appears to be back to normal after a brief outage on Sunday evening. No new outage reports were recorded on Downdetector after 8 p.m. EDT. Apple has not issued a statement explaining the cause of the disruption. Also Read:Apple hikes prices by up to 42%: Check full price list for MacBooks, iPads, Vision Pro and HomePod devices Update: By 7:45 p.m. EDT, reports of Apple Maps issues on Downdetector had climbed to 2,500. Users continued to report problems with directions and search features, while Apple had yet to comment on the outage. One Downdetector user reported seeing the message, ""Displays: Directions Not Available. Route information is not available at this moment."" Also Read:Is DoorDash down? Thousands report errors amid widespread outage; ‘something went wrong’ Initial report: Apple Maps is down for thousands of users in the United States, with many reporting issues accessing directions and search features. According to Downdetector, reports of problems started around 7:10 p.m. EDT on Sunday. By 7:30 p.m., theoutagetracking website had received more than 1,500 reports from users experiencing issues with the service. Many users also took to X (formerly Twitter) to report the outage. One user complained, ""Mannnnnnn I can’t get nowhere on the damn Easy w Apple Maps down."" Another wrote, ""Now why would Apple Maps be down when we are at the beach and have no idea where to go. Google Maps, we appreciate you at the moment but we have to work on the look of ","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 05:04:43 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/why-is-apple-maps-not-working-users-report-directions-unavailable-as-outage-hits-everything-is-down-101782689511946.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Major earthquake threat in California? San Andreas, San Jacinto faults at highest stress in 1,000 years, new study warns","Major earthquake threat in California? San Andreas, San Jacinto faults at highest stress in 1,000 years, new study warns","Major earthquake threat in California? San Andreas, San Jacinto faults at highest stress in 1,000 years, new study warns Scientists say Southern California faults are carrying record stress levels, increasing concern about a future major earthquake, but not predicting one. A new study has raised fresh concerns about earthquake risk in Southern California. Researchers say the region’s two major fault systems, the San Andreas Fault and the San Jacinto Fault are carrying more built-up stress than at any time in the last 1,000 years. The findings do not predict when an earthquake will happen, but they suggest conditions are now similar to those seen before some of the area’s largest historic quakes. The study was published in theJournal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earthand was led by Dr. Liliane Burkhard of the University of Bern in Switzerland. Earthquakes happen when stress slowly builds along faults and is suddenly released. To understand how much stress is currently stored beneath Southern California, Dr. Liliane Burkhard and her team created a physics-based model using a 1,000-year record of earthquakes. The researchers relied on geological evidence, including radiocarbon dating, tree-ring records and past earthquake data. The results were striking. According to the study, stress levels along parts of both fault systems have reached or even exceeded anything seen during the past millennium. One section of the San Jacinto Fault recorded the highest stress level found anywhere in the model, while a nearby section of the San Andreas Fault showed similarly high values. Researchers said this combination places the region in a “critically loaded” state. Also Read:Venezuela earthquake death toll reaches 1,430; rescue ops continue A major focus of ","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 04:43:17 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/major-earthquake-threat-in-california-san-andreas-san-jacinto-faults-at-highest-stress-in-1-000-years-new-study-warns-101782683295127.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Is DoorDash down? Thousands report errors amid widespread outage; ‘something went wrong’,Is DoorDash down? Thousands report errors amid widespread outage; ‘something went wrong’,"Is DoorDash down? Thousands report errors amid widespread outage; ‘something went wrong’ DoorDash is experiencing an outage, with thousands of users reporting ""something went wrong"" errors while accessing delivery services, per DownDetector. Instantdelivery appDoorDash is down for thousands of users on Sunday afternoon, according to DownDetector. Users report getting a ""something went wrong"" error message while trying to access delivery services on DoorDash. On DownDetector, which tracks such outages based on users' reports,around 6000 users are reporting issues with DoorDash. According to DownDetector, users reported a variety of issues, including the ""something went wrong"" and ""unexpected error try again"" later messages. Here's a graph of the outage reports, as seen on Down Detector: However, most of the users reported that these problems occurred when they were trying to check out their shopping carts. 38% said that the app was not working problem, while 17% reported problems with ordering. As of now, DoorDash has not issued an official update, even as hundreds of users of the popular instant-delivery platform complained online about errors when ordering. No replies are being sent out via theDoorDash Help X account, tasked with handling users' complaints on the platform. Hundreds of frustrated DoorDash users took to social media to express frustration over the outage. DoorDash has approximately 56 million monthly active users, of whom close to 60% are based in the United States. Many of them appeared on X to complain about the issues on Sunday afternoon. “DoorDash is acting up. Am I going to have to cook for myself tonight? OMG,”wrote one user. “Door Dash are you all experiencing any issues in the Raleigh Nort","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 04:22:12 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/is-doordash-down-thousands-report-errors-amid-widespread-outage-something-went-wrong-101782686853955.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"US, Iran agree to halt strikes, renew talks; Qatar meeting set for Tuesday: Report","US, Iran agree to halt strikes, renew talks; Qatar meeting set for Tuesday: Report","US, Iran agree to halt strikes, renew talks; Qatar meeting set for Tuesday: Report A return to diplomacy would follow several days of strikes and counterstrikes since an Iranian projectile hit a cargo vessel in the Strait of Hormuz on Thursday DUBAI/WASHINGTON, June 28 (Reuters) - Iran and the United States agreed to halt recent hostilities in the Gulf and renew talks regarding their dispute over the Strait of Hormuz, Axios reported on Sunday, a move that could end tit-for-tat strikes that had threatened to unravel an interim peace agreement. The two sides plan to meet Tuesday in Qatar, Axios reported, citing a senior U.S. official. Reuters could not immediately confirm the report. The White House did not immediately respond to a request for comment. A return to diplomacy would follow several days of strikes and counterstrikes since an Iranian projectile hit a cargo vessel in the Strait of Hormuz on Thursday, with both the U.S. and Iran accusing the other of breaking an interim ceasefire that was agreed to on June 17. Iran launched missiles and drones at U.S. military sites in Kuwait and Bahrain early on Sunday, shortly after PresidentDonald Trumpthreatened to wipe out the Iranian leadership if they did not stick to the agreement to end their war. Also Read:Iran issues stern warning to Trump & his Arab allies after US bombing campaign; 'Follow rules or...' Meanwhile, Israel said on Sunday it had once again struck Iran-backed armed Hezbollah militants in Lebanon, destroying underground infrastructure used by the group in a village in southern Lebanon. That came after another strike on Saturday, which closely followed its latest ceasefire deal with Lebanon on Friday to calm fighting that Iran says must end if the wid","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 03:30:30 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-iran-agree-to-halt-strikes-renew-talks-qatar-meeting-set-for-tuesday-report-101782683409901.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Pakistan says it carried out ground operation, strikes along Afghan border","Pakistan says it carried out ground operation, strikes along Afghan border","Pakistan says it carried out ground operation, strikes along Afghan border The military did not say how the strikes were carried out. ISLAMABAD (AP) —Pakistanisecurity forces Sunday carried out an “intelligence-based” ground operation along the Pakistan-Afghanistan border, followed by “calibrated strikes” against militant hideouts and safe havens, killing 29 fighters, officials said. The military did not say how the strikes were carried out, but Pakistan's air force has been involved in the previous targeting of alleged militant hideouts insideAfghanistan. In a post on X, Information Minister Attaullah Tarar said theoperation was launchedin response to multiple militant attacks across the country. There was no immediate response from Afghanistan. It comes a day after militants armed with guns and explosivestargeted the regional headquarters of the paramilitary Rangersin the southern port city of Karachi, killing three soldiers. Security forces killed three attackers and arrested another assailant, whom the military identified as an Afghan national in wounded condition. Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, a breakaway faction of the Pakistani Taliban, claimed responsibility for the Karachi attack in a statement Saturday night. Tarar said Pakistan’s latest operation along the Afghan border targeted the hideouts and safe havens of Jamaat-ul-Ahrar and Fitna al-Khwarij, a term Pakistan uses for the Pakistani Taliban. Pakistan has witnessed a surge in militant attackstargeting police and security forces in recent years. Authorities have blamed the Pakistani Taliban, known as Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan, or TTP, and allied militant gro","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 03:19:29 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/pakistan-says-it-carried-out-ground-operation-strikes-along-afghan-border-101782682743631.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,What did the USPS Postmaster General say about mail-in voting? David Steiner mail ballot policy causes waves,What did the USPS Postmaster General say about mail-in voting? David Steiner mail ballot policy causes waves,"What did the USPS Postmaster General say about mail-in voting? David Steiner mail ballot policy causes waves David Steiner said the Postal Service would stop delivering mail ballots to states that refuse to share voter data under a proposed Trump administration rule.&nbsp; The US Postal Service's proposed mail ballot policy has ignited a fresh political and legal battle ahead of the November 2026 general election. Postmaster General David Steiner told lawmakers that, under a proposed regulation, the USPS would no longer deliver mail ballots to states that refuse to provide voter eligibility data requested by the federal government. The federal government's alleged intention to overreach its jurisdiction has alarmed critics. A few judges have concurred. A group of Democratic-led states filed a lawsuit, and a federal judge on Thursday prevented the scheme from proceeding. The postmaster general's remarks raise a ""credibility issue,"" according to League of Women Voters CEO Celina Stewart. Read more:Judge blocks Donald Trump's executive orders on mail-in ballots for Washington and Oregon Appearing before the Senate Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs Committee, Steiner was asked whether the Postal Service would continue delivering mail ballots to states that refused to comply with the proposed voter data requirement. Steiner defended the proposal by saying it would help ensure “the right ballots are going to the right people.” He argued that states should want accurate voter information so ballots are delivered only to eligible voters. “I would think that states would want the information to ensure that the ballots that they think they’re sending out are the ballots that are actually getting sent out,” Stiener said. At the same hearing, howe","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 03:06:35 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/what-did-the-usps-postmaster-general-say-about-mail-in-voting-david-steiner-mail-ballot-policy-causes-waves-101782678087248.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"'What a loser': Biden unleashes blistering attack on Trump in fiery Maryland speech, slams 'brazen corruption'","'What a loser': Biden unleashes blistering attack on Trump in fiery Maryland speech, slams 'brazen corruption'","'What a loser': Biden unleashes blistering attack on Trump in fiery Maryland speech, slams 'brazen corruption' Joe Biden sharply criticized Donald Trump over corruption allegations, foreign policy, Washington projects and the January 6 Capitol riot. Former US President Joe Biden launched one of his strongest attacks yet on President Donald Trump, calling him ""a loser"" during a speech at the Maryland Democratic Party's gala on Saturday. Biden accused the Trump administration of corruption, incompetence and damaging America's global standing as Democrats intensified campaigning ahead of the November 2026 midterm elections. Biden issued a statement promoting grassroots political engagement before speaking at the dinner. ""I've always believed democracy isn't a spectator sport,"" he remarked, commending volunteers for organizing campaigns throughout communities, making phone calls, and knocking on doors. Read more:Joe Biden cancer update: Jill Biden says ‘it’s hard to be a caretaker’ as ex-President has ‘slowed down’ During his keynote speech at the ""Live! Casino & Hotel"" Maryland in Hanover, Biden attacked several of Trump's initiatives in Washington, such as the renovation of the Lincoln Memorial Reflecting Pool, plans for a triumphal arch, and the demolition of the White House East Wing for a ballroom. Biden also accused the administration of engaging in ""brazen, blatant corruption"" on an unprecedented scale. Biden went on, ""The reflecting pool project in particular, for which the federal government awarded a $1.7 million no-bid filtration system contract to a Trump donor who is a neighbor of the president's Mar-a-Lago club in Florida, reflects something even worse than the narcissism and incompetence at the core of this ad","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 02:38:52 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/what-a-loser-biden-unleashes-blistering-attack-on-trump-in-fiery-maryland-speech-slams-brazen-corruption-101782674877743.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Nancy Guthrie case: TMZ founder Harvey Levin says he offered to pay Bitcoin ransom, claims FBI asked him to 'stand down'","Nancy Guthrie case: TMZ founder Harvey Levin says he offered to pay Bitcoin ransom, claims FBI asked him to 'stand down'","Nancy Guthrie case: TMZ founder Harvey Levin says he offered to pay Bitcoin ransom, claims FBI asked him to 'stand down' Harvey Levin says TMZ was prepared to send one Bitcoin to a person claiming to know who abducted Nancy Guthrie, but FBI later asked the outlet not to proceed. TMZ founderHarvey Levinhas claimed he was prepared to pay the Bitcoin ransom to obtain alleged evidence linked to the disappearance of Nancy Guthrie, the 84-year-old mother of NBC's Today co-anchor Savannah Guthrie. Levin said he contacted the FBI after receiving messages from the same person throughout the course of Nancy's disappearance. However, he alleged the bureau later ""ghosted"" him after initially expressing interest in the proposal. Levin made the remarks while discussing a fresh series of emails sent to TMZ by the anonymous individual demanding one Bitcoin in exchange for the information, and he has spoken with TMZ using the same IP address throughout. Nancy Guthriehas been missing since late January after authorities said she was abducted from her home in Tucson, Arizona. The investigation remains active, with the FBI and the Pima County Sheriff's Department continuing to seek leads. Read more:Nancy Guthrie case update: 'Major break' on the horizon? Former FBI agent makes big claim In an interview with CBS producer Anna Schecter on Saturday, Levin explained that the sender has consistently demanded one bitcoin, worth about $60,000, in exchange for information identifying the alleged kidnappers and the location of evidence. He said, “A month ago, I called the FBI and I said, ‘Look, I just have this sense this guy might be real.’” He proposed making a documentary in which TMZ would transfer the Bitcoin while tracking its destination, but only with law enforcement's k","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 00:45:21 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/nancy-guthrie-case-tmz-founder-harvey-levin-says-he-offered-to-pay-bitcoin-ransom-claims-fbi-asked-him-to-stand-down-101782669861062.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,'Fourteen-year wait': Immigration lawyer reveals the calculation pushing Indian H-1B green card holders toward the UK,'Fourteen-year wait': Immigration lawyer reveals the calculation pushing Indian H-1B green card holders toward the UK,"'Fourteen-year wait': Immigration lawyer reveals the calculation pushing Indian H-1B green card holders toward the UK Indian H-1B professionals facing long green card delays are increasingly considering the UK while continuing to pursue their US plans. As questions continue around the future of the H-1B visa programme in the United States, many highly skilled professionals are quietly exploring another option. According to Indian-origin immigration lawyer Yash Dubal, a growing number of Indian engineers and researchers living in the US are looking at the United Kingdom’s Global Talent Visa while still keeping their long-term American plans alive. The main reason is simple: years-long green card backlogs and continued uncertainty around the US immigration system. Speaking to TheAmerican Bazaar, Yash Dubal, CEO and director of London-based A Y & J Solicitors, said his firm has seen more enquiries from Indian professionals working in the US on H-1B visas. Dubal said many of these applicants are engineers and researchers in their thirties. Most have spouses on H-4 visas and children born in the United States. While they have built their lives in America, many have spent years waiting for permanent residency. He explained that the UK’s Global TalentVisahas become attractive because it offers a much faster path to settlement. Unlike the Skilled Worker visa, it is tied to the individual and not an employer. Applicants do not need a job offer, employer sponsorship, or a minimum salary. They can change jobs, work independently, provide consultancy services, or start a business. Also Read:H-1B visa update: US envoy Sergio Gor issues key clarification on Indians amid Trump's visa rules Dubal told the publication, “The April 2026 Visa ","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 00:41:38 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/fourteenyear-wait-immigration-lawyer-reveals-the-calculation-pushing-indian-h-1b-green-card-holders-toward-the-uk-101782670718504.html,2026-06-29 02:11:33
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"What happened in Mabank, Texas? Fire Chief Charlie Woodard fatally struck by vehicle after rodeo event","What happened in Mabank, Texas? Fire Chief Charlie Woodard fatally struck by vehicle after rodeo event","What happened in Mabank, Texas? Fire Chief Charlie Woodard fatally struck by vehicle after rodeo event Mabank Fire Chief Charlie Woodard was fatally struck by a vehicle while directing traffic after the Mabank Rodeo, according to officials. Update:ABC affiliateKLTV 7 reportedthat Charlie Woodard, the fire chief of Mabank, was killed while directing traffic on Saturday night. The outlet cited the Texas Municipal Police Association, which confirmed that Chief Woodward was fatally struck by a vehicle after the Mabank Rodeo. The report noted that Woodward was also a police officer, alongside being the fire chief. Original Story:A fire official with the Mabank fire department inTexasdied in a fatal accident near the Mabank Rodeo, the Kaufman County Sheriff's Department confirmed in a post on Facebook. The Sheriff's office did not release details of how the fire official died. Also read:Major earthquake threat in California? San Andreas, San Jacinto faults at highest stress in 1,000 years, new study warns However, the incident has sparked a lot of buzz in local communities, particularly due to the lack of details from officials. Some reports claim that the fire officer was struck by an oncoming vehicle near Mabank rodeo while attending to a crash. In the update on Facebook, the Kaufman County Sheriff's Office only noted: ""Our thoughts and prayers go out to the Mabank Fire Department, Mabank PD and the family."" Also read:'Fourteen-year wait': Immigration lawyer reveals the calculation pushing Indian H-1B green card holders toward the UK But the details remain unconfirmed. It seemed a different incident from Montgomery County is being linked to Mabank, in Kaufman County, where a deputy with the Montgomery County Sheriff","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 23:54:23 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/what-happened-in-mabank-texas-kaufman-county-sheriffs-office-mourns-fire-officers-fatal-accident-101782669589210.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Winthrop fire: Evacuations ongoing around Hamilton Ranch Loop as wildfire spreads; check map, visuals","Winthrop fire: Evacuations ongoing around Hamilton Ranch Loop as wildfire spreads; check map, visuals","Winthrop fire: Evacuations ongoing around Hamilton Ranch Loop as wildfire spreads; check map, visuals A wildfire near Winthrop, Washington, prompted Level 3 evacuations as flames threatened homes. Level 2 warnings remain in effect for the town and nearby areas. Update:According to incident command, the forward progress of the fire has been slowed down but not entirely stopped. The evacuation orders still remain in effect, as of 4:45 ET. Smoke from the fire has significantly decreased, reports indicate. Original Story:Awildfirein the area of Hamilton Ranch Loop and Westside Chewuch Road in Winthrop,Washington, on Sunday afternoon. A Level 3 evacuation has been ordered in the nearby area as residents have been asked to immediately move out with the fire threatening residential structures.According to WatchDuty, the fire is 50 acres. A Level 3 evacuation was issued in the areas of Hamilton Ranch Road, Black Bird Road, High Ridge Road and Hamilton Ranch Loop Road, according to the incident command, per Watch Duty. Meanwhile, a Level 2 warning was issued for the Town of Winthrop and further towards east. Also read:Why is Apple Maps not working? Users report directions unavailable as outage hits; 'everything is down' The Washington Fire's incident command is calling the wildfire 'Hamilton Fire' because of its proximity to the Hamilton Ranch Loop. According to WatchDuty, the fire is located right off the intersection of the Hamilton Ranch Loop and the Westside Chewuch Road. The location is just north of Methow Valley, at the intersection of Highway 20 and Uplands Road. Here's a map of the fire, as seen on WatchDuty: Winthrop is a small town in north-central Washington, in Okanogan County, near the eastern slopes of the North Cascades. It f","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 22:44:07 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/winthrop-wa-fire-evacuations-ongoing-around-hamilton-ranch-loop-as-wildfire-spreads-check-map-visuals-101782666493515.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Kentucky flash flood: Metcalfe, Cumberland, and Clinton still under warning; videos show scary water level surge","Kentucky flash flood: Metcalfe, Cumberland, and Clinton still under warning; videos show scary water level surge","Kentucky flash flood: Metcalfe, Cumberland, and Clinton still under warning; videos show scary water level surge Flash flooding hit south-central Kentucky after up to 10.7 inches of rain in some areas. It inundated homes and roads as the storm moved into Tennessee. CentralKentuckysaw large amounts of rainfall Saturday, leading to flash flooding in many areas, which has caused the death of at least four people, so far. On Monday, the rainfall-causing weather conditions moved south towards Tennessee. As it moved, rain fell in south-central Kentucky, and combined with the rainfall in the last 24 hours, it led to severeflash floodingrisks in at least three counties in south-central Kentucky: Metcalfe, Cumberland, and Clinton counties. According toNWS, more than eight inches of rain have fallen overnight in these areas. It has resulted in a massive flood risk. Some areas, such as near Monticello, may have received up to 10.7 inches of rainfall overnight. More rain is falling now, even as the storm moves into Tennessee. Meanwhile, videos of the situation in south central Kentucky surfaced. It showed residential areas fully submerged under water amid the water level surge. Here's a video from South Central Kentucky showing structural damage, car submerged, houses flooded and downed powerlines as flood waters surged. Also read:4 dead amid flood caused by heavy rains, state of emergency declared : Kentucky governor The National Weather Service Office in Louisville, Kentucky, shared photos of the mesonet on normal day and on Sunday morning after over eight inches of rainfall since 12:00am local time Sunday. The photo showed the area completely inundated as NWS asked residents to urgently ""seek higher ground."" Here's the photo: A Flash Flood Emerg","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 21:49:45 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/kentucky-flash-flood-update-metcalfe-cumberland-and-clinton-under-warning-videos-show-scary-water-level-surge-101782662662628.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"What is Article 5 of the US-Iran MoU, how it is behind the latest strikes | Explained","What is Article 5 of the US-Iran MoU, how it is behind the latest strikes | Explained","What is Article 5 of the US-Iran MoU, how it is behind the latest strikes | Explained The fragile Iran-US peace deal is under fresh strain after both countries exchanged military strikes over the weekend. A fragile peace agreement between the United States and Iran is already under strain. Days after signing a Pakistan-brokered Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) on June 17, the two sides exchanged strikes across the Persian Gulf over the weekend. The latest escalation has put Article 5 of the agreement under the spotlight. Both Washington and Tehran have accused each other of violating the MoU, particularly the provision governing the reopening and management of the Strait of Hormuz. The attacks, involving commercial vessels, US strikes on Iranian targets and Tehran's retaliation against American bases in the Gulf, have exposed different interpretations of the same clause. Track news on Iran and US live here The provision lays out the framework for restoring commercial navigation through theStrait of Hormuz, a key maritime route that carries roughly one-fifth of global oil and liquefied natural gas supplies during normal times. Under the agreement, Iran committed to facilitating the safe passage of commercial ships without charging transit fees for 60 days. It also agreed to remove military and technical obstacles, including mines, within 30 days so that shipping could gradually resume. The provision also says that Tehran will hold discussions with Oman and other Gulf littoral states on the future administration and maritime services of the strait, while respecting international law and the rights of coastal states. ""Upon the signing of this MOU, the Islamic Republic of Iran wi","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 21:45:36 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/what-is-article-5-of-the-us-iran-mou-how-it-is-behind-the-latest-strikes-explained-strait-of-hormuz-abbas-araghchi-trump-101782660693165.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"117 dog remains found at California 'no-kill' shelter, many with gunshot wounds","117 dog remains found at California 'no-kill' shelter, many with gunshot wounds","117 dog remains found at California 'no-kill' shelter, many with gunshot wounds Using ground-penetrating radar, investigators located 117 intact animal remains buried in an open field. The remains of at least 117 dogs, many with apparent gunshot wounds, have been found at aCalifornia""no-kill"" animal shelter, prompting an investigation into allegations of animal cruelty, fraud, and conspiracy. The Humboldt County Sheriff's Office said on Friday that investigators also recovered 21 canine skulls, hundreds of bones and other remains during searches at Miranda's Rescue Animal Sanctuary, a 50-acre facility in Fortuna, California, the Associated Press (AP) reported. During a search on Thursday, investigators identified an area inside a barn where they believe dogs were likely killed. More than 600 dog collars were found nearby, according to the sheriff's office. Humboldt County Sheriff William Honsal described the site as a ""horrific scene."" No charges have been filed so far. The investigation began in April after the sheriff's office received what it described as credible information regarding allegations of felony animal abuse, animal cruelty, fraud and conspiracy. Also Read:Days after LAPD dog shooting, Norfolk cop shoots pooch dead while investigating separate incident – what we know Using ground-penetrating radar, investigators located 117 intact animal remains buried in an open field in various stages of decomposition. X-rays conducted on 70 of the remains found evidence of bullet fragments in many of them, with authorities saying gunshot wounds appeared to be the cause of death in several cases. Additional remains in advanced stages of decomposition were a","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 20:54:44 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/117-dog-remains-found-at-california-no-kill-shelter-many-with-gunshot-wounds-101782658404058.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Pakistan watchdog suspends Geo News for 15 days over 'offensive' religious broadcast,Pakistan watchdog suspends Geo News for 15 days over 'offensive' religious broadcast,"Pakistan watchdog suspends Geo News for 15 days over 'offensive' religious broadcast The regulator said Geo News aired ""religious visualisations"" during a June 26 programme that could offend religious sentiments. Pakistan's media regulator has suspended the broadcast licence of television channel Geo News for 15 days over content aired during a programme marking Muharram, one of the most sensitive periods in the Islamic calendar. The regulator said Geo News aired ""religious visualisations"" during a June 26 programme that could offend religious sentiments, undermine religious harmony and disturb public order. Also Read |6 terrorists killed after Pakistan army base attack in Karachi, 4 soldiers dead Geo News has apologised and issued a statement on Sunday saying the material had been aired in error and did not reflect the channel's editorial position or beliefs. Depictions of the Prophet Mohammed and other revered Islamic figures are a highly charged issue in Pakistan, where mass protests have broken out over cartoons published in Western countries. Religious disputes can quickly trigger public anger in the Muslim-majority country, where authorities impose heightened security during Muharram commemorations. Geo News, one of Pakistan's largest private television channels, said the content in question had been removed from all its platforms. It said the footage showed rituals practised by some people in Iraq and the Middle East and was intended to show local customs, not endorse any religious view. Pakistan's media regulator said Geo had failed to exercise editorial caution and directed it to conduct an internal inquiry. The matter was also referred to the regulator's Council of Complaint","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 19:52:00 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/pakistan-watchdog-suspends-geo-news-for-15-days-over-offensive-religious-broadcast-101782656229559.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Over 1,300 excess deaths linked to Europe heatwave: WHO calls heat a ‘silent killer’","Over 1,300 excess deaths linked to Europe heatwave: WHO calls heat a ‘silent killer’","Over 1,300 excess deaths linked to Europe heatwave: WHO calls heat a ‘silent killer’ French health officials said there had been around 1,000 more deaths than expected in that country in just three days. The World Health Organization said Sunday that over 1,300 excess deaths had been recorded in Europe since June 21 in connection with the record-breaking heatwave roasting much of the continent. Tens of millions have been braving a weekend of extreme temperatures in Europe as a deadly heatwave moves eastwards, with some countries announcing rising death tolls and health services warning of saturation. ALSO READ |Where will Europe’s heatwave be most deadly? On Sunday morning, French health officials said there had been around 1,000 more deaths than expected in that country just since Wednesday. And across Europe, ""more than 1,300 excess deaths have been recorded since 21 June linked to high temperatures in Europe"", WHO chief Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said on X. ""Heat stress is often called the 'silent killer' - and European homes, workplaces and schools were not built for these temperatures,"" he said. ALSO READ |Europe heat wave: Why Paris banned drinking liquor and what alcohol does to the body At least 191 million people are forecast to endure temperatures of at least 35C on Sunday in Europe, with the heat particularly intense in Germany, the Czech Republic, Hungary and Poland, according to AFP estimates. A total of 381 million people in Europe, excluding Turkey, will see temperatures surpass 30C, according to analysis based on forecasts from the German Meteorological Service and 2025 population projections from the Joint Research Centre collated by Austrian NGO Klimadashboa","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 19:16:54 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/over-1-300-excess-deaths-linked-to-europe-heatwave-who-calls-heat-a-silent-killer-101782653847283.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Putin says Russia is going through ‘difficult period’ amid Ukraine war, vows to overcome challenges","Putin says Russia is going through ‘difficult period’ amid Ukraine war, vows to overcome challenges","Putin says Russia is going through ‘difficult period’ amid Ukraine war, vows to overcome challenges Putin said the hardships faced by the country had made it stronger and provided valuable lessons. Russian PresidentVladimir Putinon Sunday said Russia is going through a ""difficult period"" but expressed confidence that the country would overcome its challenges, ensure national security, and continue strengthening its borders. Speaking at the conference of the ruling United Russia party ahead of parliamentary elections scheduled for September, Putin said the hardships faced by the country had made it stronger and provided valuable lessons, according to Russian state news agency TASS. ""We are going through a difficult period, but it has taught us much,"" Putin said, while wishing the United Russia party success in the upcoming parliamentary elections. Also Read:The Russian Economy Looks More Vulnerable Each Day His remarks come as Russia continues to faceUkrainian droneand missile strikes, alongside Western sanctions imposed over the war in Ukraine. Also Read: Addressing the United Russia party congress, Putin said the government was aware of the challenges confronting the country and was taking steps to address them. ""Yes, we see the problems, we are aware of them and are responding to them, but we will certainly ensure the security of both the country and our citizens, as well as the inviolability of Russia's borders,"" he said. Putin also vowed that Russia would overcome the threats it currently faces. ""We will undoubtedly overcome all the challenges facing us today, including terrorist attacks on our territory and infrastructure facilities,"" he added. (With inputs from AF","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 19:15:12 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/putin-says-russia-is-going-through-difficult-period-amid-ukraine-war-vows-to-overcome-challenges-101782653431234.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Saudi Aramco helicopter crashes in Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura, 14 killed","Saudi Aramco helicopter crashes in Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura, 14 killed","Saudi Aramco helicopter crashes in Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura, 14 killed The cause of the helicopter crash is yet to be determined, and an investigation is ongoing. A helicopter belonging to Saudi Arabian oil giant Aramco crashed in the Kingdom's Ras Tanura, killing 14 people, the Saudi state news agency reported on Sunday. The cause of the helicopter crash is yet to be determined, and an investigation is ongoing. Ras Tanura is home to the largest refinery in the West Asian region, and it is operated bySaudi Aramco. According to a report by the Saudi Press Agency, all the people killed in the crash, which happened on Sunday morning at 6am, were citizens of the Kingdom ofSaudi Arabia. The Saudi ministry of energy expressed its condolences to the families of the people killed in the crash. “The ministry of energy extends its deepest condolences and sincere sympathies to the families of the deceased, praying that Almighty God grants them mercy and forgiveness and accepts them among the martyrs. Indeed, we belong to God, and to Him we shall return,” the ministry said in a statement carried by SPA. Aramco had resumed crude oil loadings on Friday at its Ras Tanura terminal in the Gulf after they were halted for nearly four months. Saudi Arabia, the world's biggest oil exporter, has joined ‌a rush to move cargoes after West Asian producers ramped up oil and gas output and exports ahead of an interim deal to halt thewar between the United States and Iran. In another harrowing incident,eleven people have died after a civilian aircraft carrying a group of skydivers crashedon Sunday in Tomblaine, near Nancy inFrance, as per Reuters. The victims are","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 17:21:15 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/saudi-aramco-helicopter-crashes-in-saudi-arabias-ras-tanura-death-toll-cause-101782647133931.html,2026-06-29 02:11:34
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Civilian plane carrying 11 skydivers crashes in France's Tomblaine; all dead,Civilian plane carrying 11 skydivers crashes in France's Tomblaine; all dead,"Civilian plane carrying 11 skydivers crashes in France's Tomblaine; all dead All 11 people, including five instructors, five students and the pilot, were killed, authorities said. Eleven people have died after a civilian aircraft carrying a group of skydivers crashed on Sunday in Tomblaine, near Nancy in France, as per Reuters. The victims are believed to include five instructors, five students and the pilot, according to French outlet Le Parisien. Emergency services rushed to the scene as authorities worked to assess the situation. The deceased are believed to be self-employed nurses from Nancy who were participating in their first skydiving experience, Thierry Pechey, president of the Meurthe-et-Moselle branch of the Order of Independent Nurses, told BFMTV from the crash site. ""It would appear that the victims are self-employed nurses,"" he said. Several relatives of the victims witnessed the crash, according to local media. They had gathered to watch the group's introductory skydiving experience. Authorities have set up a medical and psychological support unit to assist the victims' families. ALSO READ |Aramco helicopter crashes in Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura, 14 killed Theplane crashednear houses, on a grassy area alongside a road. ""The accident happened around 11 am,"" a witness told the French daily L'Est Républicain. ""I was driving by on my way to do my shopping at Auchan when I saw it dive and crash."" The aircraft was a German-registered Pilatus, a type of plane commonly used for parachute ops and skydiving activities, local media reported. ALSO READ |Small plane crashes into Beijing's tallest building, casualties feared Representatives from the pr","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 17:02:15 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/plane-crashes-in-frances-tomblaine-casualties-feared-rescue-underway-deatl-toll-101782645989386.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Two state elections may break Malaysia’s ruling coalition,Two state elections may break Malaysia’s ruling coalition,"Two state elections may break Malaysia’s ruling coalition Anwar Ibrahim’s long-sought tenure as prime minister may be cut short On his path to power, Anwar Ibrahim spent nearly ten years in jail on politicised corruption and sodomy charges. Now the man who has been prime minister since November 2022 is experiencing confinement of another kind. Over three years into the job that he coveted for so long, the walls are closing in on the veteran of Malaysian politics. Mr Anwar leads a coalition with a supermajority in parliament, but his partners are eyeing the exits. Two upcoming state elections look likely to bring down his government if the results do not go his way. The voting in Johor, a southern data-centre powerhouse, and Negeri Sembilan, a smaller state farther up the peninsula, will not affect the make-up of the federal parliament. But the coalition partners are testing their strength by running against each other in both states. One of them, the United Malays National Organisation (UMNO), was long Malaysia’s ruling party before losing power in 2018. Its members have bristled at playing second fiddle to Mr Anwar in his multi-party coalition. Worse, it has meant joining hands with their arch-rivals, the majority-Chinese Democratic Action Party (DAP), the largest single party in the coalition. UMNO holds itself up as the guardian of Malaysia’s longstanding affirmative action policies showered upon the majority Malay ethnic group, while the DAP argues for more meritocracy. If UMNO does well in the state polls, that could give it the confidence to withdraw from the coalition at the federal level and g","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 16:15:55 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/two-state-elections-may-break-malaysia-s-ruling-coalition-101782643412940.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Airlines are installing new luxury seats, but no one is allowed to sit in them","Airlines are installing new luxury seats, but no one is allowed to sit in them","Airlines are installing new luxury seats, but no one is allowed to sit in them Lie-flat seats in private pods face long waits for safety certifications, delaying debuts for carriers’ high-end offerings. Dutch airline KLM has been preparing for a new and more-luxe business-class experience, featuring 34 lie-flat seats equipped with privacy doors and 19-inch touch screens. But there’s a problem. The seats, which the carrier is marketing to high-end fliers on a brand new long-haul jet, haven’t yet been certified by aviation authorities. So when it launches its inaugural flight featuring the seats in September, they will be empty. Lufthansa has had a similar problem. So does Singapore Airlines. A number of carriers, including United and American, have debuted new business-class suites with their doors locked open, awaiting approval. Airlines are in an amenities arms race to stand apart and fly passengers in comfort and style. Seats are at the forefront, with private pods kitted out with extra storage space, wireless charging, ottomans and retractable privacy dividers. U.S. and European aviation safety authorities are saying not so fast. Seats are often the last things installed on a new plane. In some cases, the lengthy process left plane makers with jets they can’t yet deliver to airlines. “We have airplanes sitting for customers, completely done, waiting for seat certifications,” Boeing Chief Executive Kelly Ortberg said last month. Everything from seat-belt buckle mechanisms to door latches could change how passengers might evacuate in an emergency, or how they are protected in a crash, Federal Aviation Administration chief Bryan Bedford told reporters last month. “You don’t ","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 16:10:22 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/airlines-are-installing-new-luxury-seats-but-no-one-is-allowed-to-sit-in-them-101782643098494.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Checks and Balance newsletter: The World Cup doesn’t understand America,Checks and Balance newsletter: The World Cup doesn’t understand America,"Checks and Balance newsletter: The World Cup doesn’t understand America John Prideaux, our executive editor, on why America is a spectacular place to host the football tournament This is the introduction to Checks and Balance, a weekly, subscriber-only newsletter bringing exclusive insight from our correspondents in America. Sign up forChecks and Balance Fans of soccerball love to complain about America. With its unbridled capitalism, it has the gall to treat football as a business. “Hydration breaks” have been introduced in this year’s World Cup. Fans suspect these are the first step to more frequent advert breaks for TV viewers, a grubbily commercial innovation that changes their game. Going to a matchcosts a fortune. Dynamic pricing may work in the NFL or NBA, where the market in tickets resembles the market for any other luxury good. But soccer, with its roots in teams of men who worked in the same factory, has different values. In economics terms, these countries consider football to be part of the commons. In many of the big football countries there is an expectation that the games will be carried by channels that are available to everyone. Were the rights sold to the highest bidder, in profit-maximising fashion, and large numbers of people unable to watch, that would become an existential issue for the government of the day. There’s the fact that Donald Trump’s America would seem singularly disinterested in being hospitable. Foreign fans who followed the operations of ICE in Minneapolis earlier this year wondered whether America actually wanted the world to visit. Then its government denied entry to Omar Artan, one of Africa’s best referees, si","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 16:07:46 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/checks-and-balance-newsletter-the-world-cup-doesn-t-understand-america-101782642804667.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Europe bakes under record heat: 1,000 excess deaths in France, Germany hits record 41.7°C | Top points","Europe bakes under record heat: 1,000 excess deaths in France, Germany hits record 41.7°C | Top points","Europe bakes under record heat: 1,000 excess deaths in France, Germany hits record 41.7°C | Top points France has recorded 1,000 excess deaths during the blistering heatwave sweeping Europe. Most of the deaths involved people aged 65 and older. A blistering heatwave sweeping across Europe has shattered temperature records, strained public health systems, and disrupted daily life across multiple countries. From record-breaking heat in Scandinavia and the Alps to emergency alerts in Italy and emergency responses in Germany and France, the extreme weather has triggered deaths, infrastructure stress, and unusual measures as authorities struggle to cope with rising temperatures. Here are the latest updates in Europe heatwave:","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 16:04:01 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/europe-bakes-under-record-heat-1-000-excess-deaths-in-france-hottest-day-in-denmark-top-points-101782640487171.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,‘I will return’: Sheikh Hasina sets a timeline for Bangladesh return two years after ouster,‘I will return’: Sheikh Hasina sets a timeline for Bangladesh return two years after ouster,"‘I will return’: Sheikh Hasina sets a timeline for Bangladesh return two years after ouster Sheikh Hasina said that she doesn't fear death, pointing out the 1975 assassination of her father Sheikh Mujibur Rehman. Almost two years after she was driven out of her country and sought refuge in India, former Bangladesh Prime MinisterSheikh Hasinaadopted a defiant tone on her return, saying she would go back “this year”. In an interview with NDTV, Hasina alleged that the verdict against her was an attempt to make her party, theAwami League, “leaderless”, but she said such attempts will not succeed. Bangladesh's International Crimes Tribunal found Hasina guilty of crimes against humanity and sentenced her to death in November last year. “The verdict against me is not justice. It is part of an illegal, unconstitutional, and politically motivated process. The judiciary has been turned into an instrument of political revenge to make the Awami League leaderless. Such attempts have been made before. They failed then, and they will fail again,” she said in the interview. Hasina said that she doesn't fear death, pointing out the assassination of her father, Sheikh Mujibur Rehman, credited as the founder ofBangladesh, and the loss of her family in 1975. She asserted that she would be back in Bangladesh “this year”. “I do not fear death. In 1975, I lost my parents, my brothers, and almost my entire family. On 21 August, there was an attempt to kill me with grenades. Many conspiracies have been hatched against me. But breaking through every web of conspiracy, I stood beside the people of Bangladesh. I was elected Prime Minister five times by the people's vote and worked for the country's unprecedented","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 14:25:45 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/i-will-return-sheikh-hasina-sets-a-timeline-for-bangladesh-return-two-years-after-ouster-101782636577774.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Israel says it killed Hezbollah militants in south Lebanon days after interim peace deal,Israel says it killed Hezbollah militants in south Lebanon days after interim peace deal,"Israel says it killed Hezbollah militants in south Lebanon days after interim peace deal The Israeli military said it struck the structure from which the militants operated and dismantled a rocket launcher that posed a threat. Israel on Sunday said it it killed Hezbollah militants armed with rocket-propelled grenades just a few days after signing a trilateral agreement with US and Lebanon to end the war in the Middle east country. It also claimed to have struck a rocket launcher in the Nabatieh area of southern Lebanon to remove threats to its soldiers. Track latest updates on US-Iran war The Israeli military said it struck the structure from which the militants operated and dismantled a rocket launcher that posed a threat, Reuters reported. As Israel launched strikes in Lebanon despite a deal, Iran called ""these brutal attacks... a blatant violation"" of the interim truce deal. Hezbollah leader Naim Qassem rejected the agreement to end the conflict which has also threatened to derail the wider US-Iran peace effort. Also read:Operation Amistad: Indian assistance reaches Venezuela after massive earthquake devastation This attack comes day after Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu on Saturday hailed the US-brokered agreement with Lebanon, calling it a historic achievement that dealt a blow to arch-foes Iran and Hezbollah. ""Yesterday ... we achieved a historic deal for the state of Israel after direct negotiations between Israel and Lebanon,"" Netanyahu said in a televised briefing. ""This is a blow to Iran and Hezbollah."" Notably, Hezbollah has criticised the agreement and Qassem declared it void. The agreement was signed in Washington on Friday after five rounds of talks. It aims to pave the way ","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 12:29:33 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/israel-says-it-killed-hezbollah-militants-in-south-lebanon-days-after-interim-peace-deal-101782625099136.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Iraq deploys security forces in Baghdad's Green Zone which houses US Embassy,Iraq deploys security forces in Baghdad's Green Zone which houses US Embassy,"Iraq deploys security forces in Baghdad's Green Zone which houses US Embassy The Green Zone typically hosts the US embassy and other diplomatic missions, as well as international institutions and government offices Iraqi security forces deployed inBaghdad'sfortified Green Zone early Sunday with the raid targeting ""several politicians"", a security official told AFP. The Green Zone houses the US embassy and other diplomatic missions, as well as international institutions and government offices. High-ranking officials and politicians also live there. Also Read IShelly Kittleson: American journalist released after weeklong abduction in Iraq; Kataib Hezbollah says she must… Video footage shared on local Telegram channels showed security forces in heavy vehicles such as tanks in the Green Zone, including a clip of them inside a compound and, in one instance, inside a house. A security official told AFP, under the condition of anonymity, that a ""raid targeted several politicians overfinancial corruption, per judicial orders,"" adding that the operation involved anti-terrorism forces and the military. No official statement has been released as yet. Also Read IUttarakhand CM Dhami orders tough action in Haridwar land scam The source did not provide further details or names of officials reportedly arrested. The large-scale raids were aimed at arresting senior Iraqi officials, with several names circulating in local media reports. Also Read IIsrael built base in Iraq to aid Iran war, struck Iraqi troops to keep them away: Report Iraq'snew Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidivowed to fight corruption and mismanagement that has plagued Iraq for decades.","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 11:59:59 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/iraq-deploys-security-forces-in-baghdads-green-zone-which-houses-us-embassy-101782618058961.html,2026-06-29 02:11:35
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Venezuela earthquake death toll reaches 1,430; rescue ops continue","Venezuela earthquake death toll reaches 1,430; rescue ops continue","Venezuela earthquake death toll reaches 1,430; rescue ops continue Tensions have flared between civilian and aid rescue teams as the 72-hour window to recover people alive narrows Tensions flared Saturday as desperation grew inVenezuela'sstate ofLa Guairaas rescuers and civilians searched for earthquake survivors and the death toll rose sharply to 1,430. Families reported at least 68,900 people missing Saturday, three days after the one-two punch of 7.2 and 7.5 magnitudeearthquakesdevastated the South American nation. Venezuelans looking for loved ones and neighbors used shovels, heavy equipment, ropes and bare hands atop mounds of toppled concrete throughout La Guaira, one of the hardest-hit states. They were joined by a growing number of international rescue teams who began to climb through the rubble, offering a small glint of hope to anguished families. Tensions peaked over what many Venezuelans viewed as an inadequate response by the government, whose soldiers, firefighters, police and military cadets were evidently underprepared to respond to the scope of the tragedy. Frustration was amplified by efforts to project the image of a robust state response. Aid agencies consider the first 48 to 72 hours as crucial for retrieving people alive, though that can be extended if they have access to food and water. Venezuelan officials said 17 flights carrying more than 1,600 rescue team members had touched down by Saturday. As 72 hours passed since the earthquakes struck, many felt every minute ticking away as they ran out of time to rescue people alive. Also Read IVenezuela earthquakes, in graphics: Ruptures, sideways collision & tremors that tore through ","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 11:17:37 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/venezuela-earthquake-death-toll-reaches-1-430-rescue-ops-continue-101782619273970.html,2026-06-29 02:11:36
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Ballistic missile attack rocks Kyiv, Ukraine issues warning","Ballistic missile attack rocks Kyiv, Ukraine issues warning","Ballistic missile attack rocks Kyiv, Ukraine issues warning Ukrainian military has requested civilians to stay indoors as air defense forces operate in the region following repeated strikes by Moscow A ballisticmissileattack is underway inKyiv, the Ukrainian military said early on Sunday. ""Air defence forces are operating in the capital. Remain in shelters!"", the capital's mayor Vitali Klitschko said on Telegram. AFP journalists heard explosions and saw several flashes in the sky. Also Read ITank lid blown off, plumes of black smoke: Dramatic video captures Ukrainian strike on Moscow refinery | Watch Russianstrikes a day earlier in Dnipropetrovsk in central-easternUkraineand the northern Sumy region killed two people. It also comes after Ukraine launched attacks on Volgograd and Belgorod in Russia's southwest, and Horlivka, in the Donetsk region of Ukraine, which is controlled by Moscow. Also Read IInside the Race to Save One of Ukraine’s Holiest Sites From a Russian Strike Three people were killed in the attacks, regional authorities said. Russia has targeted Ukraine with near-daily strikes since the launch of itsfull-scale invasionin February 2022, the bloodiest conflict in Europe since the Second World War. Also Read IThe G7 has nudged open a window for diplomacy in Ukraine Ukraine has also stepped up its strikes in recent months, which it describes as retaliatory, against Russia and the territories occupied by Moscow.","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 09:10:43 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/ballistic-missile-attack-rocks-kyiv-ukraine-issues-warning-101782612876023.html,2026-06-29 02:11:36
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Trump nominates former US Marine Lance Schroyer as new director of ICE, first pick to hold job permanently","Trump nominates former US Marine Lance Schroyer as new director of ICE, first pick to hold job permanently","Trump nominates former US Marine Lance Schroyer as new director of ICE, first pick to hold job permanently Lance Schroyer has been nominated by President Trump as the first pick to hold the position of ICE director permanently in his second term PresidentDonald Trumpsaid he will nominate Lance Schroyer as director of US Immigration and Customs Enforcement, his first pick to hold the job permanently in his second term. Trump said in a social-media post on Saturday that Schroyer is a former Oklahoma state trooper and US Marine. “He is a PATRIOT with real operational experience, and proven leader with DECADES of experience locking up the worst of the worst,” Trump wrote. Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin lauded the pick, writing on social media that Schroyer is “coming straight from the operational field where he ran large scale operations.” Mullin representedOklahomain the US Senate. Also Read IWho is Lance Schroyer? Meet Trump's pick for the next ICE director In early June, Mullin told attendees at the National Sheriff Association’s annual conference that he hired Schroyer at theDepartment of Homeland Securityto help enable more partnerships between the federal government and local police for immigration enforcement. Mullin added Schroyer is a “good friend” and joined DHS soon after he retired as a major in charge of Oklahoma Highway Patrol’s tactical team. ICE has not had a permanent director since 2017, when Trump picked Tom Homan, now his border czar, to run the agency. The Senate never acted on the confirmation and Homan asked for his nomination to be withdrawn in 2018. Schroyer is the first person Trump has nominated to head the immigration agency in his second term.Todd Lyonswas the acting head of ICE","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 09:10:11 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/trump-nominates-former-us-marine-lance-schroyer-as-new-director-of-ice-first-pick-to-hold-job-permanently-101782612887790.html,2026-06-29 02:11:36
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"Explosions, 90-minute gun battle: How Karachi terror attack that killed 4 Pakistani soldiers unfolded","Explosions, 90-minute gun battle: How Karachi terror attack that killed 4 Pakistani soldiers unfolded","Explosions, 90-minute gun battle: How Karachi terror attack that killed 4 Pakistani soldiers unfolded Terrorists stormed the Sindh Rangers compound after an explosives-laden vehicle rammed into the building, breaching the premises before attackers opened fire At around 8:30 pm on Saturday, an explosives-laden vehicle rammed into a Karachi building belonging to Pakistan's paramilitary force, the Sindh Rangers. Following the blast, terrorists entered the compound and began firing indiscriminately while hurling hand grenades,triggering more explosions. The Rangers responded immediately, leading to an exchange of fire between security personnel and the attackers. The assailants belonged to Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, a militant faction of the banned Tehreek-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP), said reliable security forces, according to PTI. Rescue 1122 Sindh said it received reports of an explosion near Gulistan-e-Jauhar Block 5 and immediately dispatched emergency teams from its central command and control centre. Video |Loud gunfire, explosion in Karachi; Rangers HQ under attack in Pakistan's biggest city? In response to the attack, authorities sealed off the Rangers compound and the surrounding roads. Residents were instructed to remain indoors, while some nearby neighbourhoods experienced power outages during the operation. A gun battle lasting around 90 minutes then ensued. According to PTI, the attackers and Pakistani security forces exchanged fire for about 90 minutes. Special Security Unit (SSU) commandos and the Anti-Terrorist Force (ATF) joined Rangers personnel in the operation, killing six terrorists and capturing one injured attacker. Four Rangers soldiers were killed in the assault. Sindh Inspector General of Police Javed Alam Odho said an e","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 09:09:27 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/how-karachi-terror-attack-on-sindh-rangers-that-killed-4-pakistani-soldiers-unfolded-jamaat-ul-ahrar-taliban-101782616015543.html,2026-06-29 02:11:36
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"6.1-magnitude earthquake hits Japan's Iwate coast; no tsunami risk, says JMA","6.1-magnitude earthquake hits Japan's Iwate coast; no tsunami risk, says JMA","6.1-magnitude earthquake hits Japan's Iwate coast; no tsunami risk, says JMA The JMA said the earthquake was caused by a reverse fault with a pressure axis running in the west-northwest to east-southeast direction. A strong earthquake of magnitude 6.1 struck off the coast of Iwate Prefecture in northeastern Japan early Sunday, the Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) said, adding that there is no risk of a tsunami from the quake. According to the JMA, the earthquake occurred at 5:21 am (local time) at a depth of 41 kilometres. Track live updates on US-Iran war The quake registered a maximum seismic intensity of lower 5 (5-) on Japan's seismic intensity scale in Hachinohe City, Aomori Prefecture, and Fudai Village, Iwate Prefecture. Tremors ranging in intensity from 4 to 1 were felt across areas extending from Hokkaido to the Kanto-Koshin region. Also read:US strikes 10 targets in Iran, Tehran launches attacks on Kuwait, Bahrain: Latest escalation in West Asia war The agency said the earthquake was caused by a reverse fault with a pressure axis running in the west-northwest to east-southeast direction. As of 7:00 am (local time), no additional earthquakes with a seismic intensity of 1 or higher had been recorded following the event. The JMA noted that the earthquake occurred in the same area as the June 25 offshore Iwate earthquake, which recorded a maximum seismic intensity of 6 . Also read:Muggy heat kicks Delhi's ‘feel like’ temperature to 51°C, highest this year; rainfall likely from Monday Authorities warned that areas affected by the June 25 quake should remain alert for earthquakes of up to seismic intensity 6 for about a week from the earlier event. The agency also cautioned that re","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 09:00:48 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/japan-earthquake-iwate-coast-no-tsunami-risk-jma-casualties-damage-101782616957340.html,2026-06-29 02:11:36
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,Donald Trump health: New book claims president changed meeting locations due to hearing concerns,Donald Trump health: New book claims president changed meeting locations due to hearing concerns,"Donald Trump health: New book claims president changed meeting locations due to hearing concerns A new book by White House correspondents Maggie Haberman and Jonathan Swan claims Donald Trump changed where he held meetings due to hearing issues and fatigue. A new book has brought back PresidentDonald Trump's health into focus, alleging he altered the location of White House meetings to accommodate age-related hearing issues. The claims appear in ‘Regime Change: Inside the Imperial Presidency of Donald Trump’, written by veteran White House reporters Maggie Haberman of The New York Times and Jonathan Swan of The Atlantic. According to the book, Trump's hearing has declined in addition to his apparent memory loss, swelling and bruises. However, the White House has dismissed the allegations and has maintained that Trump remains in ""excellent"" health. Read more:Trump health update: Major drug that POTUS has taken for years missing from medical records, raising serious concerns The book is based on more than 1,000 interviews conducted over two years. The authors say their reporting is based on interviews with administration officials, advisers and people familiar with Trump's presidency. According to the interviews conducted by Haberman and Swan, Trump increasingly held meetings in the Oval Office instead of the East Wing because theOval Officeoffered better acoustics and allowed him to remain seated. The authors also claim the president occasionally asked people to repeat questions because of hearing difficulties and experienced moments of fatigue. “He was having trouble hearing, asking people to repeat questions they had just asked,” they wrote. The book also alleges that some aides privately remarked that Trump ""seems old"" and tha","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 08:45:02 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-news/donald-trump-health-new-book-claims-president-changed-meeting-locations-due-to-hearing-concerns-101782611179389.html,2026-06-29 02:11:36
Hindustan Times,India (Mainstream),en,"US strikes 10 targets in Iran, Tehran launches attacks on Kuwait, Bahrain: Latest escalation in West Asia war","US strikes 10 targets in Iran, Tehran launches attacks on Kuwait, Bahrain: Latest escalation in West Asia war","US strikes 10 targets in Iran, Tehran launches attacks on Kuwait, Bahrain: Latest escalation in West Asia war The latest exchange of attacks risks escalating the conflict in West Asia once again, despite Iran and the United States reaching an interim agreement. TheUS militarycontinued its aggression against Iran for the second day straight as it said on Saturday that it carried out strikes on 10 targets inIranafter attack on a commercial ship passing through theStrait of Hormuz. In response, Iran's navy and aerospace forces launched a joint missile and drone operation targeting US military sites inKuwaitandBahrain. The latest exchange of attacks risks escalating the conflict in West Asia once again, despiteIran and the United Statesreaching an interim agreement aimed at negotiating a final deal to end the war.Follow US-Iran war live updates here. US central command said in a social media post that American military aircraft struck Iranian military ""surveillance infrastructure, communication systems, air defence sites, drone storage facilities, and minelayer capabilities"" after a merchant vessel came under attack early on Saturday. It later confirmed that the operation targeted 10 Iranian military sites at different locations in and around the Strait of Hormuz. The US Central Command also shared a video of the strikes on Saturday. The command said that Iranian forces attacked the oil tanker Kiku using a one-way drone. The vessel was carrying more than two million barrels of crude oil while passing through the Strait of Hormuz. The US military said ""Iran had a chance to honour the ceasefire agreement"" but ""elected not to"" when it carried out the attack on the Kiku. ""Commercial vessel transits through the Strait of Hormuz continue. US ","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 08:08:05 +0530",https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/us-strikes-10-targets-in-iran-tehran-launches-attacks-on-kuwait-bahrain-latest-escalation-in-west-asia-war-irgc-trump-101782612371426.html,2026-06-29 02:11:36
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Taliban says Pakistani strikes in eastern Afghanistan kill, wound dozens of civilians","Taliban says Pakistani strikes in eastern Afghanistan kill, wound dozens of civilians","Taliban says Pakistani strikes in eastern Afghanistan kill, wound dozens of civilians The Taliban government said Pakistani airstrikes in eastern Afghanistan killed and wounded dozens of civilians, condemning the attacks as a ""cowardly act of aggression"" The Taliban government on Monday accused Pakistan of carrying out airstrikes in eastern Afghanistan that it said killed and wounded dozens of civilians. According toTalibangovernment spokesman Zabihullah Mujahid, the strikes targeted the provinces of Paktika, Paktia and Kunar. In a post on X, Mujahid condemned the military action, describing it as a “cowardly act of aggression”. Pakistan’s security forces launched a ground offensive along the Afghanistan border on Sunday before carrying out what officials described as “calibrated strikes” on militant hideouts and safe havens, an operation that authorities said killed 29 fighters. Information Minister Attaullah Tarar said in a post on X that the operation was launched in response to a series of militant attacks across Pakistan. Pakistan has seen a sharp rise in attacks targeting police and security personnel in recent years, with Islamabad blaming the Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP) and allied militant groups for much of the violence. The operation came a day after heavily armed militants stormed the regional headquarters of the paramilitary Rangers in the southern port city of Karachi, killing three soldiers. Security forces later killed three attackers and captured another wounded assailant, whom the military identified as an Afghan national. A breakaway faction of the Pakistani Taliban, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, claimed responsibility for the Karachi attack in a statement Saturday night. The operation comes less than three weeks aft","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 07:25:47 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/taliban-says-pakistani-strikes-in-eastern-afghanistan-kill-wound-dozens-of-civilians-14027245.html,2026-06-29 02:11:37
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"New generation of US nuclear reactors 'up & running' in Idaho, backers call it turning point for the industry","New generation of US nuclear reactors 'up & running' in Idaho, backers call it turning point for the industry","New generation of US nuclear reactors 'up & running' in Idaho, backers call it turning point for the industry A new generation of small nuclear reactors is up and running — or nearly so — in the United States, in what backers are calling a turning point for the industry. A new generation of small nuclear reactors is up and running — or nearly so — in the United States, in what backers are calling a turning point for the industry. The milestone, made possible by billions in private and government funding, was on display in the middle of the Idaho desert, where a cluster of drab hangars might otherwise go unnoticed. But the presence of heavily armed soldiers, security checkpoints, and signs warning of radioactivity are anything but ordinary. It was here, at the Idaho National Laboratory (INL), that startup Antares on June 4 became the first company to run a new-design nuclear reactor in the US in nearly 50 years. “This is the first real moment in this new nuclear renaissance,” said Jordan Bramble, CEO of Antares. Aalo Atomics, another participant in the program launched in 2025 under President Donald Trump, is set to do the same in the coming days — also here in Idaho, just hours before a presidential target date: July 4 and the nation’s 250th anniversary. Meanwhile, on June 18, another startup, Valar Atomics, hit the same milestone in Utah, reaching what is known as criticality — the point at which a reactor can sustain its own nuclear chain reaction. After developing more than 50 reactor prototypes — including the world’s first to feed electricity into the grid, in 1951 — INL had pressed pause following accidents at Three Mile Island in the US and Chernobyl in current day Ukraine. Then came the war in Ukraine, followed by the AI boom — puttin","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 07:23:43 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/new-generation-of-us-nuclear-reactors-up-running-in-idaho-backers-call-it-turning-point-for-the-industry-14027242.html,2026-06-29 02:11:37
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Germany, Poland, and Czechia record hottest temperatures as Europe reels with intense heatwaves","Germany, Poland, and Czechia record hottest temperatures as Europe reels with intense heatwaves","Germany, Poland, and Czechia record hottest temperatures as Europe reels with intense heatwaves As the continent of Europe grapples with intense heatwaves, Germany, Czechia, Poland and Hungary reached a record temperature of more than 40 °C on Sunday. As the continent ofEurope grapples with intense heatwaves, Germany, Czechia, Poland and Hungary reached a record temperature of more than 40 °C on Sunday. The dramatic heatwaves that have already been linked to hundreds of deaths in western Europe are now spreading east. More than 191 million people in Europe are now witnessing temperatures of at least 35 °C, with extreme heat warnings across the region.Germanyon Sunday recorded a new all-time high temperature of 41 °C in Coschen, located near the Polish border in eastern Brandenburg. According to the preliminary data of the national weather service, the Sunday temperature broke the previous record of 41.5 °C set a day earlier in Drewitz. Amid the heatwaves in Gohrischheide, in eastern Germany, a fire broke out in a forest contaminated with ammunition from the Second World War. The high temperature further complicated the firefighters’ efforts. 🇩🇪 It's so hot in Germany that Berlin has hit 41°C.The city has water trucks driving around, spraying major parks to help cool things down.Berlin forgot it's in Germany.Writer: Daniyalpic.twitter.com/yr0FWUcqOZ Apart from this, a major firefighting operation was also paused at a former munitions disposal site near the village of Traisen in south-west Germany. According to the German news agencyDPA, the firefighters had to briefly pause after explosions were triggered, and a bomb disposal unit was brought in. At least 650 people in Traisen had to evacuate their homes due to the fire. Me","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 07:10:31 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/germany-poland-and-czechia-record-hottest-temperatures-as-europe-reels-with-intense-heatwaves-14027238.html,2026-06-29 02:11:37
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Indian contingent marches on Seychelles National Day, PM Modi stands to honour armed forces","Indian contingent marches on Seychelles National Day, PM Modi stands to honour armed forces","Indian contingent marches on Seychelles National Day, PM Modi stands to honour armed forces Seychelles' President honoured Prime Minister Narendra Modi with the special presidential distinction, 'Guardian of the Blue Horizon', recognising his contributions to bilateral ties and regional cooperation Prime Minister Narendra Modi stood to honour the Indian Army and Navy contingents as they marched during Seychelles’ 50th Independence Day celebrations on Sunday, highlighting India’s participation in the Golden Jubilee event during his three-day visit to the island nation. The Indian Army and Indian Navy contingents participated in the ceremony marking the50th anniversaryof the East African country’s independence. During the march, the army contingent sang the famous regimental song, ‘Badlu Ram Ka Badan’, drawing attention at the event. WATCH || Indian Army's Assam Rifles contingent and Indian Navy marching band participate in Seychelles' Golden Jubilee celebrations. The INS Tarkash contingent, including 29 personnel led by Sub Lt Nag Dev, represents the Indian Navy.@PMOIndia@MEAIndia@MIB_India@PIB_India…pic.twitter.com/fZ6vXpbml7 Prime Minister@narendramodiattended the National Day celebrations marking the 50th anniversary of Independence of Seychelles as the Guest of Honour, becoming the first Indian Prime Minister to be accorded this honour.An Indian marching contingent comprising Navy and Army…pic.twitter.com/zbmu6dLJOa Modi is on a three-day official visit toSeychellesthat began on Saturday. During the visit, he is holding discussions with the country’s leadership and taking part in events commemorating 50 years of Seychelles’ independence. India’s contingent at the celebrations included personnel from the Assam Regiment of the Indian Army, the Indian Navy’s frontline s","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:49:33 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pm-modi-stands-to-honour-indian-armed-forces-contingent-during-seychelles-national-day-celebrations-14027240.html,2026-06-29 02:11:37
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Globe at a glance: From US, Iran agree to halt strikes to Europe battling heatwaves","Globe at a glance: From US, Iran agree to halt strikes to Europe battling heatwaves","Globe at a glance: From US, Iran agree to halt strikes to Europe battling heatwaves Your window to world news: While the United States and Iran agreed to halt hostilities, Pakistan on Sunday launched fresh strikes against the Afghan border. All this, and much more in today's edition of Globe at a Glance. PM Modi’s Seychelles visit -On the final day of his Seychelles visit, Prime Minister Narendra Modi is attending the National Day celebrations marking the 50th anniversary of Independence of Seychelles as the Guest of Honour. He became the first Indian Prime Minister to be accorded this honour. An Indian marching contingent comprising Navy and Army teams participated in the parade. An Indian Naval band also formed part of the Indian marching unit, underlining the close and special India-Seychelles partnership. US-Iran agree to halt hostilities again -The United States and Iran have agreed to halt military strikes and will meet Tuesday in Doha, Qatar, to resolve tensions over the Strait of Hormuz, according to US officials, who shared the details with Axios on the condition of anonymity. The talks, originally intended to focus on Iran’s nuclear program, will instead centre on ensuring safe commercial shipping through the Strait of Hormuz. Officials also plan to discuss implementing a direct military hotline between the US and Iran to prevent future incidents. Pakistan carries out strikes against Afghanistan -Pakistani security forces Sunday carried out a ground operation along the Pakistan-Afghanistan border, followed by “calibrated strikes” against militant hideouts and safe havens, killing 29 fighters, officials said. In a post on X, Information Minister Attaullah Tarar said the operation was launched in response to multiple militant attacks across the country. There was no i","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:29:32 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/globe-at-a-glance-from-us-iran-agree-to-halt-strikes-to-europe-battling-heatwaves-14027227.html,2026-06-29 02:11:37
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Critical rescue window closing in Venezuela as twin earthquake death toll nears 1,500","Critical rescue window closing in Venezuela as twin earthquake death toll nears 1,500","Critical rescue window closing in Venezuela as twin earthquake death toll nears 1,500 Emergency teams with rescue dogs were searching Sunday for any remaining survivors of powerful twin earthquakes that struck Venezuela, where the death toll has surpassed 1,450 and nearly 200 building have completely collapsed. Emergency teams with rescue dogs were searching Sunday for any remaining survivors of powerful twin earthquakes that struck Venezuela, where the death toll has surpassed 1,450 and nearly 200 building have completely collapsed. A man and his teen son were found alive under the rubble Sunday by French and American rescue teams in Caraballeda, a town about 40 kilometers north of Caracas, AFP journalists saw. The rescue offered a glimmer of hope in an ongoing tragedy that has shaken a country already mired in an economic crisis, but tens of thousands of people were still reported missing and the critical 72-hour window for rescuing trapped victims following a natural disaster has now passed. Millions more people were feared to lack sanitation and other basic needs after one of Latin America’s most devastating earthquake disasters. Rescue teams from the United States, Mexico and elsewhere scrambled to save people as desperate residents dug by hand for relatives trapped in the pancaked layers and rubble of collapsed apartments. Some 774 buildings were badly damaged in back-to-back quakes of magnitude 7.2 and 7.5 that struck on Wednesday evening, including 189 buildings that have totally collapsed, National Assembly President Jorge Rodriguez said Sunday. In one of the worst-hit areas, the coastal city of La Guaira, Hector Aguilera came to search for four family members buried in the rubble. “We don’t have the support to get our family out – we can’t do it alone. They are buried ther","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:14:59 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/critical-rescue-window-closing-in-venezuela-as-twin-earthquake-death-toll-nears-1500-14027236.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"US, Iran agree to halt attacks and resume Hormuz talks, US official says","US, Iran agree to halt attacks and resume Hormuz talks, US official says","US, Iran agree to halt attacks and resume Hormuz talks, US official says The United States and Iran have agreed to pause hostilities in the Gulf and resume talks over the Strait of Hormuz, raising hopes of preserving a fragile interim peace deal The United States and Iran have agreed to pause their recent military confrontation in the Gulf and resume negotiations over the Strait of Hormuz, a US official said on Sunday, in a move that could preserve a fragile interim peace agreement after days of escalating tit-for-tat strikes. According to the official, both sides have agreed to stand down while technical discussions continue on all aspects of the 14-point memorandum of understanding (MOU) signed on June 17, under which the strategically vital Strait of Hormuz was to be reopened for maritime traffic. Axios, which first reported the pause in fighting, citing a senior US official, said negotiations are expected to resume in Qatar on Tuesday. The renewed diplomatic initiative follows several days of escalating strikes after an Iranian projectile struck a cargo vessel in the Strait of Hormuz on Thursday. Since then, both Washington and Tehran have accused each other of violating the interim ceasefire agreed on June 17. In the early hours of Sunday, Iran launched missiles and drones at US military sites in Kuwait and Bahrain, shortly after President Donald Trump warned that the Islamic Republic would cease to exist if it failed to honour the agreement ending the war. Earlier, the US military said it had carried out another round of strikes on Iran, hours after a tanker was hit in the Strait of Hormuz, the world’s most important energy shipping route, which Tehran has largely closed for most of the conflict. “There may com","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:14:20 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-iran-agree-to-halt-gulf-hostilities-and-resume-hormuz-talks-14027234.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,China says it can endure an EU trade freeze. Here's why Beijing sounds so confident,China says it can endure an EU trade freeze. Here's why Beijing sounds so confident,"China says it can endure an EU trade freeze. Here's why Beijing sounds so confident China says it can withstand even a freeze in trade ties with the European Union as Brussels hardens its stance on subsidies, market access and supply-chain risks. Here's why Beijing believes it has the upper hand despite rising tensions China has issued one of its strongest warnings yet to the European Union, declaring that it can withstand a further deterioration, or even a complete freeze, in economic and trade ties if Brussels continues treating negotiations as a “mere formality”. The unusually blunt message, delivered through Yuyuantantian, a social media account affiliated with China Central Television (CCTV), comes just days before a crucial meeting between EU Trade Commissioner Maros Sefcovic and Chinese Commerce Minister Wang Wentao in Brussels. But beyond the rhetoric lies a bigger question: Why does Beijing suddenly appear so confident about risking a rupture with one of its biggest trading partners? The answer lies in a combination of shifting global trade patterns, China’s growing manufacturing dominance, Europe’s economic vulnerabilities and Beijing’s belief that it now holds more leverage than it did just a few years ago. For decades, access to the European market was considered indispensable for Chinese exporters. Today, Beijing appears to believe the equation has changed. The CCTV-linked account argued that many Chinese companies no longer view Europe as their highest-priority overseas destination, suggesting that the bloc’s influence over Chinese investment decisions has diminished. China also points to the fact that investment projects between the two sides continue despite political tensions. European countries remain eager to attract Chinese investments in electric vehicles, battery plant","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 06:04:48 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/business/china-eu-trade-freeze-beijing-confidence-europe-trade-deficit-14027230.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Pakistan launches border operation, says 29 militants killed near Afghanistan","Pakistan launches border operation, says 29 militants killed near Afghanistan","Pakistan launches border operation, says 29 militants killed near Afghanistan Pakistan said its security forces killed 29 militants in a ground operation and calibrated strikes along the Afghanistan border, days after a deadly attack on a Rangers headquarters in Karachi Pakistani security forces Sunday carried out a ground operation along the Pakistan-Afghanistan border, followed by “calibrated strikes” against militant hideouts and safe havens, killing 29 fighters, officials said. In a post on X, Information Minister Attaullah Tarar said the operation was launched in response to multiple militant attacks across the country. There was no immediate response from Afghanistan. Pakistan has witnessed a surge in militant attacks targeting police and security forces in recent years. Authorities have blamed the Pakistani Taliban, known as Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan, or TTP, and allied militant groups for most of theviolence. It comes a day after militants armed with guns and explosives targeted the regional headquarters of the paramilitary Rangers in the southern port city of Karachi, killing three soldiers. Security forces killed three attackers and arrested another assailant, whom the military identified as an Afghan national in wounded condition. Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, a breakaway faction of the Pakistani Taliban, claimed responsibility for the Karachi attack in a statement Saturday night. Tarar said Pakistan’s latest operation along the Afghan border targeted hideouts and safe havens of the Pakistani Taliban. The Pakistani Taliban are a separate militant group from the Afghan Taliban, although the two are allies. The Afghan Taliban returned to power in neighboring Afghanistan in 2021. The latest operations are likely to further strain already tense re","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 05:39:44 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pakistan-launches-border-operation-says-29-militants-killed-near-afghanistan-14027228.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Putin admits fuel shortages. Why Ukraine's drone war is becoming Russia's costliest challenge,Putin admits fuel shortages. Why Ukraine's drone war is becoming Russia's costliest challenge,"Putin admits fuel shortages. Why Ukraine's drone war is becoming Russia's costliest challenge Ukraine's escalating drone attacks on Russian oil refineries are exposing new vulnerabilities in Moscow's war economy. As Vladimir Putin admits fuel shortages for the first time, here's why Kyiv's long-range strategy could become Russia's costliest challenge Russia has spent more than four years absorbing Western sanctions, battlefield losses and diplomatic isolation. But it is now confronting a different kind of pressure — one that is increasingly coming from hundreds of Ukrainian drones targeting the country’s oil infrastructure. President Vladimir Putin on Sundaypublicly acknowledgedfor the first time that Russia was experiencing “a certain deficit” of fuel, even as he insisted the shortages were temporary and would not derail Moscow’s military campaign in Ukraine. The admission came hours after Ukrainian drones set another major Russian oil refinery ablaze, continuing Kyiv’s expanding campaign against Russia’s energy industry. “Our ’long-range sanctions’ reached two oil refineries in Russia,” Zelenskyy wrote on Telegram after the overnight strikes. “Each strike means a reduction in the resources that fuel the Russian war machine, and another step toward peace.” For much of the war, Ukraine’s objective was straightforward: defend territory and slow Russian advances along the front. Now, Kyiv is increasingly trying to make the war economically unsustainable for Moscow. Last week, Zelenskyy announced what he described as a “40-day influence operation” to be carried out by Ukraine’s long-range strike units, saying Russian military logistics across occupied Ukrainian territories had been “severely hindered.” Rather than focusing solely on military bases, Ukraine is striking oil refineries, fuel storage facilities, transport hubs a","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 05:29:38 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/business/putin-admits-fuel-shortages-why-ukraines-drone-war-is-becoming-russias-costliest-challenge-14027225.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,PM Modi attends Seychelles National Day celebrations as Guest of Honour,PM Modi attends Seychelles National Day celebrations as Guest of Honour,"PM Modi attends Seychelles National Day celebrations as Guest of Honour Indian naval assets and military contingent join Seychelles’ landmark celebrations as the two countries deepen strategic and environmental cooperation Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Sunday attended the Golden Jubilee celebrations of Seychelles’ National Day as the Guest of Honour. Modi is on a three-day official visit to Seychelles that began on Saturday, during which he is holding discussions with the country’s leadership and participating in events marking 50 years of Seychelles’ independence. An Indian military contingent took part in the celebrations, including personnel from the Assam Regiment of the Indian Army, the Navy’s frontline stealth frigate INS Tarkash and the indigenously built Survey Vessel Large INS Ikshak. An Indian Navy marching contingent and naval band were also part of the ceremonial events. Seychelles gained independence from Great Britain on June 29, 1976, ending over 160 years of British administration. Claimed by France in 1756 and named Sechelles, the islands were later surrendered to the British in 1810 and officially ceded in the 1814 Treaty of Paris. In 1903, Seychelles was made a separate British Crown Colony. “The first country in the Indian Ocean region that I visited as Prime Minister was Seychelles in 2015… I came here because I believe that Seychelles occupied a special place in India’s vision for the Indian Ocean. As I return here after a decade, that conviction is stronger than ever. I’m delighted to join you as you celebrate 50 years of your independence,” PM Modi said in his address to the Parliament.“The Indian Ocean does not separate, but connects India and Seychelles,” he underlin","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 00:29:38 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pm-modi-attends-seychelles-national-day-celebrations-as-guest-of-honour-14027202.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Israel says soldier killed in south Lebanon during clash with Hezbollah militant,Israel says soldier killed in south Lebanon during clash with Hezbollah militant,"Israel says soldier killed in south Lebanon during clash with Hezbollah militant The incident came just two days after Israel and Lebanon signed an agreement aimed at reducing hostilities along their shared border. An Israeli soldier was killed during fighting in southern Lebanon on Sunday, the Israeli military said, just two days after Israel and Lebanon signed an agreement aimed at ending hostilities along the border. The military identified the soldier as Captain David Hazutt, 21, a platoon commander, who was killed during an operation in southern Lebanon. According to the Israeli military, Hazutt was killed after Israeli troops entered a suspicious structure in the Deir Seryan area and encountered a Hezbollah militant. An additional Israeli soldier was lightly injured during the incident. “Following the incident, the soldiers began searching for the terrorist and struck targets in the area,” a military official said. Later, the military said the Hezbollah fighter had been located in a nearby building and was killed by Israeli forces. Separately, Lebanon’s state-run National News Agency reported that Israeli forces carried out another attack on the outskirts of the southern towns of Deir Seryan and Taybeh on Sunday. The reported strike came despite the recently signed agreement intended to reduce cross-border hostilities. With Hazutt’s death, Israel’s military has lost 38 soldiers and one civilian contractor since the conflict with Hezbollah began in early March, according to the Israeli military. First Published:June 29, 2026, 00:14 IST First Published:June 29, 2026, 00:14 IST Israeli-made defence systems, including aircraft protection and pilot helmet technology, have been supplied","Mon, 29 Jun 2026 00:14:09 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/israel-says-soldier-killed-in-south-lebanon-during-clash-with-hezbollah-militant-14027205.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Israel sold advanced weapons systems to Qatar, Saudi despite no diplomatic ties: Report","Israel sold advanced weapons systems to Qatar, Saudi despite no diplomatic ties: Report","Israel sold advanced weapons systems to Qatar, Saudi despite no diplomatic ties: Report The report noted that Qatar’s Emir Sheikh Tamim bin Hamad Al Thani travelled to Tehran last year aboard one of the aircraft equipped with the Israeli-made defence system. Advanced Israeli-made defence systems, including aircraft protection technology and pilot helmet systems for F-15 fighter jets, have been supplied to Qatar and Saudi Arabia despite the absence of formal defence ties with either country, according to a report published on Sunday. The report byHaaretz, based on publicly available photographs and defence procurement documents, said the equipment was delivered through contracts linked to US defence manufacturers. According to the report, three of the 11 aircraft operated by Qatar’s royal family were fitted with Elbit Systems’ C-MUSIC airborne defence system between 2020 and 2022 while undergoing maintenance in Basel, Switzerland. The report noted that Qatar’s Emir Sheikh Tamim bin Hamad Al Thani travelled to Tehran last year aboard one of the aircraft equipped with the Israeli-made defence system. The C-MUSIC system is designed to protect aircraft from shoulder-fired surface-to-air missiles. It uses radar to detect incoming threats and a high-powered laser to deflect them. The technology is also installed on Israel’s official government aircraft, Wings of Zion, as well as the French presidential aircraft. The report said procurement documents linked to the 2017 US-Qatar agreement for the sale of F-15QA Ababil fighter jets showed that Israeli companies secured subcontracts worth between $150 million and $250 million. The contracts reportedly included the supply of 160 Joint Helmet Mounted Cueing Systems (JHMCS), valued at around $","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 23:55:30 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/israel-sold-advanced-weapons-systems-to-qatar-saudi-despite-no-diplomatic-ties-report-14027191.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Tehran-Dubai flights to resume after weeks of disruption during Iran conflict,Tehran-Dubai flights to resume after weeks of disruption during Iran conflict,"Tehran-Dubai flights to resume after weeks of disruption during Iran conflict Air links between Iran and the UAE to restart as the region moves toward restoring normal travel following months of military tensions and airport disruptions Flights between Tehran and Dubai are set to restart on Monday, Iranian state media reported, marking the reopening of a key air route weeks after the two countries were affected by the wider West Asia conflict. Ramin Kashefazar, head of Tehran’s Imam Khomeini International Airport, said arrangements had been completed to resume services between Tehran and Dubai. “The necessary arrangements have been made to reopen the Tehran-Dubai route at Imam Khomeini Airport,” Iranian state television and other media outlets quoted him as saying. Tickets for a Monday morning flight operated by Iranian airline Sepehran were available for purchase, with the aircraft scheduled to depart Tehran at 10:40 am local time (0730 GMT). The resumption comes after Dubai’s aviation sector faced repeated disruptions during the conflict, when the airport was targeted by drone attacks and temporarily suspended operations on several occasions. Footage from one incident earlier in the war showed a drone crashing near the passenger terminal area, raising security concerns over one of the world’s busiest international aviation hubs. Iran had launched missile and drone strikes across Gulf countries in response to US and Israeli attacks, though Tehran said its operations were directed at American-linked targets and not civilian facilities. Before the conflict, Dubai International Airport was the world’s busiest airport for international passengers. However, passenger traffic dropped by 66% year-on-year during th","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 23:30:16 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/tehran-dubai-flights-to-resume-after-weeks-of-disruption-during-iran-conflict-ws-e-14027195.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,'Difficult period': Putin vows Russia will overcome challenges as Ukraine ramps up attacks,'Difficult period': Putin vows Russia will overcome challenges as Ukraine ramps up attacks,"'Difficult period': Putin vows Russia will overcome challenges as Ukraine ramps up attacks Putin said the country was passing through a “difficult period” but expressed confidence that Russia would overcome all challenges, as Ukraine continued to launch drone strikes on Russian territory. Russian President Vladimir Putin on Sunday acknowledged that the country was going through a “difficult period” but said Russia would overcome all challenges as Ukraine intensified drone strikes deep inside Russian territory. Speaking at the congress of the ruling United Russia party ahead of September’s parliamentary elections, Putin said the hardships facing the country had made it stronger and pledged to ensure Russia’s security despite the continuing attacks. “We are going through a difficult period, but it has taught us much,” Putin said, according to Russian state news agency TASS. The Russian president said Moscow was aware of the challenges posed by the conflict and would continue to respond to them. “Yes, we see the problems, we are aware of them and are responding to them, but we will certainly ensure the security of both the country and our citizens, as well as the inviolability of Russia’s borders,” Putin said. “We will undoubtedly overcome all the challenges facing us today, including terrorist attacks on our territory and infrastructure facilities,” he added. Putin’s remarks came as Ukraine claimed responsibility for new drone attacks targeting Russian energy infrastructure. According to Krasnodar Governor Veniamin Kondratyev, a Ukrainian drone strike killed one person in Russia’s southern Krasnodar region and sparked a fire at an oil refinery. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said the attacks were aimed at weakening Russia’s ability to sustain its milita","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 22:57:02 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/difficult-period-putin-vows-russia-will-overcome-challenges-as-ukraine-ramps-up-attacks-14027188.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,'What a loser': Joe Biden launches blistering attack on Trump two years after last debate,'What a loser': Joe Biden launches blistering attack on Trump two years after last debate,"'What a loser': Joe Biden launches blistering attack on Trump two years after last debate Biden made the remarks during a Democratic Party event in Maryland, nearly two years after leaving the White House. Former US President Joe Biden on Saturday launched a sharp attack on President Donald Trump, calling him “a loser” and accusing his administration of corruption, incompetence and putting personal vanity ahead of governance. Speaking at a gala hosted by the Maryland Democratic Party in Hanover, Biden criticised a series of projects undertaken by the Trump administration in Washington, DC, including changes to the White House East Wing, plans for a triumphal arch and the renovation of the Lincoln Memorial Reflecting Pool. Biden said Trump’s efforts to reshape the nation’s capital reflected misplaced priorities and poor governance. Referring to the administration’s decision to compensate people convicted over their roles in the January 6, 2021 attack on the US Capitol before they were later pardoned by Trump, Biden remarked, “Whoa! What a loser.” Biden alleged that the renovation of the Lincoln Memorial Reflecting Pool highlighted what he described as widespread corruption within the administration. He criticised the awarding of a no-bid contract for the project’s filtration system to a Trump donor and neighbour of Trump’s Mar-a-Lago resort. “It’s the corruption — the corruption, the brazen, blatant corruption. Corruption on a scale never seen before in American history in any administration,” Biden said. The former president also criticised Trump’s approach to foreign policy, particularly his relationship with Russian President Vladimir Putin following Russia’s invasion of Ukra","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 22:50:14 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/what-a-loser-joe-biden-launches-blistering-attack-on-trump-two-years-after-last-debate-14027187.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Seychelles Opposition leader praises India’s 'unity in diversity', urges leadership to draw inspiration","Seychelles Opposition leader praises India’s 'unity in diversity', urges leadership to draw inspiration","Seychelles Opposition leader praises India’s 'unity in diversity', urges leadership to draw inspiration His remarks came after Prime Minister Narendra Modi addressed an Extraordinary Sitting of the National Assembly in Victoria, where he underscored the shared values of parliamentary democracy and bilateral cooperation. Leader of Opposition in the Seychelles National Assembly Bernard Georges on Sunday praised the depth of India–Seychelles relations, calling it a strong example of “unity in diversity,” and urged his country’s leadership to draw inspiration from New Delhi. Speaking after Prime Minister Narendra Modi addressed an Extraordinary Sitting of the National Assembly in Victoria, Georges said the engagement reflected the inclusive spirit of democratic governance. “The political head of the largest democracy taking the time to speak at length and with passion to the members of one of the world’s smallest underlines the universal nature of the democratic process and the enduring relevance of parliamentary and participatory democracy as a system of governance,” he said. Seychelles Opposition Leader to PM Modi:The political direction in Seychelles is putting our democracy at risk. My hope is that your presence in Seychelles will encourage our leaders to look to India as an example of unity in diversity.pic.twitter.com/FNAl5ET7ES He added that Modi’s visit would encourage Seychellois leaders to view India as a model of unity amid diversity. Georges, however, also used the occasion to highlight political divisions within Seychelles, expressing concern over the domestic political climate. He said that while the country was celebrating its Golden Jubilee of independence in the presence of global dignitaries, the opposition would be absent from official ceremonies due to internal political diff","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 21:40:39 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/seychelles-opposition-leader-praises-indias-unity-in-diversity-urges-leadership-to-draw-inspiration-14027167.html,2026-06-29 02:11:38
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Europe heatwave halts Leipzig trams as soaring temperatures damage tracks,Europe heatwave halts Leipzig trams as soaring temperatures damage tracks,"Europe heatwave halts Leipzig trams as soaring temperatures damage tracks The European heatwave has reached a dangerous tipping point as extreme temperatures melt tram track beds in Leipzig, trigger over 2,300 deaths and force massive cultural cancellations across the continent. Europe’s latest heatwave has moved beyond soaring temperatures to become a test of the continent’s ability to cope with climate-driven extremes. Record heat has disrupted daily life, strained health systems and damaged urban infrastructure, with Germany’s Leipzig becoming a symbol of the crisis after suspending tram services when extreme temperatures damaged tracks and rail infrastructure. The current spell has affected millions across the continent, with Germany, the Czech Republic, Hungary and Poland among the worst-hit regions. Scientists have warned that the growing frequency and intensity of European heatwaves are closely linked to human-driven climate change, with fossil fuel emissions increasing the likelihood of longer and more severe periods of extreme heat. In Leipzig, authorities halted tram operations after intense heat caused problems with tracks and rail components. The extreme temperatures affected asphalt and bitumen surfaces, forcing transport officials to suspend services until safety checks could be completed. An Indian man in Germany showing the extreme heat condition there at 35 degree Celsius.Btw, notice the railway tracks , there is shit 💩 everywhere beside the track , reminds me of railway tracks in India before 2014 !!pic.twitter.com/idhxMx4E6z The disruption reflects a wider challenge facing European cities: infrastructure designed for historical climate conditions is increasingly vulnerable to record-breaking temperatures. Roads, railway lines, powe","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 21:37:56 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/europe-heatwave-halts-leipzig-trams-as-extreme-heat-damages-tracks-14027163.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,In photos: Europe battles scorching summer heat and record-breaking temperatures,In photos: Europe battles scorching summer heat and record-breaking temperatures,"In photos: Europe battles scorching summer heat and record-breaking temperatures Europe is facing an intense heatwave, with temperatures soaring across much of the continent and placing growing pressure on public services and infrastructure. France has been among the worst-affected countries, reporting around 1,000 excess deaths. Heatwaves are becoming more frequent, longer-lasting, and more intense in Europe. Communities in southern and western-central Europe will be most at risk of heat-related illness and death in coming years. Scientists suggest that increasingly intense heatwaves are becoming more frequent across Europe due to climate change. Research indicates that between 2000 and 2021, Europe experienced 18 severe heatwaves, compared to five between 1950 and 1999. European cities are adapting to rising heat through various measures. In Paris, wedding halls and public spaces are being converted into emergency air-conditioned rooms. Public swimming has been allowed in parts of the Canal Saint-Martin in Paris due to rising temperatures. First Published:June 28, 2026, 20:43 IST First Published:June 28, 2026, 20:43 IST Iran warns that any interference in the Strait of Hormuz will escalate tensions and delay reopening, urging adherence to the Iran-US memorandum of understanding. Iran claims sole responsibility for managing the Strait and expects it to return to pre-war capacity within 30 days. Recent US airstrikes on Iranian sites followed an Iranian drone attack on a cargo ship; Iran retaliated by targeting US force locations.","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 20:50:39 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/in-photos-europe-battles-scorching-summer-heat-and-record-breaking-temperatures-14027143.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,A truce in tatters: Iran-US clashes over Hormuz push fragile ceasefire toward collapse,A truce in tatters: Iran-US clashes over Hormuz push fragile ceasefire toward collapse,"A truce in tatters: Iran-US clashes over Hormuz push fragile ceasefire toward collapse Iran’s retaliatory strikes on US military facilities in the Gulf have pushed a fragile ceasefire closer to collapse as Washington and Tehran trade accusations and tensions mount over control of the Strait of Hormuz. The highly fragile truce brokered just weeks ago to halt a devastating West Asia conflict has violently unravelled. In a dramatic dawn escalation, Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) launched targeted missile and drone strikes against major United States military installations in Kuwait and Bahrain. The attacks hit the Ali al-Salem airbase in Kuwait and the strategic US Fifth Fleet naval base at Port Salman in Bahrain. Tehran framed the operation as a direct, “crushing response” to consecutive days of American bombardments on Iranian territory. However, the international community views this crossfire as a catastrophic fracturing of the Pakistan-mediated memorandum of understanding (MoU) signed in mid-June. With both Washington and Tehran trading severe accusations of ceasefire violations, the conflict has entered a highly volatile, unpredictable phase that threatens to engulf the entire region in a much broader war. At the absolute center of this military rupture is the Strait of Hormuz, a critical maritime chokepoint through which one-fifth of the world’s petroleum and liquefied natural gas flows. Under the mid-June agreement, Tehran had conceded to guarantee the free, uncharged passage of commercial vessels for a strict 60-day window. Yet, the peace quickly deteriorated when the US military launched retaliatory airstrikes on Saturday against Iranian coastal radar and drone storage facilities, citing an Iranian drone assault on the Panama-flagged oil tanker Kiku","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 20:35:24 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/iran-us-clashes-over-hormuz-push-fragile-ceasefire-toward-collapse-14027108.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Iran says interference in Hormuz control will escalate tensions and delay reopening,Iran says interference in Hormuz control will escalate tensions and delay reopening,"Iran says interference in Hormuz control will escalate tensions and delay reopening Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi urged all parties to honour the US-Iran memorandum of understanding (MoU), saying Tehran alone was responsible for managing the crucial shipping route under the agreement. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi on Sunday warned that any attempt to challenge Tehran’s control over the Strait of Hormuz would escalate regional tensions and delay the reopening of the strategic waterway. He also urged all parties to adhere to the memorandum of understanding (MoU) signed between Iran and the US. Amid rising tensions over the Strait of Hormuz, Araghchi said the waterway would return to its pre-war operating capacity within 30 days and insisted that Iran alone was responsible for its management under the terms of the MoU. “The Strait of Hormuz will return to its pre-war capacity within 30 days and no other body or country bears any responsibility in this regard under the memorandum of understanding signed between Iran and the United States,” he said. “I urge all parties not to interfere in the management of the Strait of Hormuz or in the arrangements being made by the Islamic Republic of Iran to reopen the Strait. They should abide by the signed memorandum of understanding and not allow it to be derailed,” Araghchi added. The Iranian foreign minister said it was the responsibility of US President Donald Trump and his administration to help de-escalate the situation by ensuring that hostilities end and commitments under the agreement are implemented. “It is the responsibility of the US government, under the commitment it accepted in the memorandum of understanding on its own behalf and on behalf of the Zionist regime, to stop the regime’s attacks, allow","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 19:45:38 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/iran-says-interference-in-strait-of-hormuz-control-will-escalate-tensions-and-delay-reopening-14027118.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Israel recognises Armenian genocide, deepening rift with Turkey","Israel recognises Armenian genocide, deepening rift with Turkey","Israel recognises Armenian genocide, deepening rift with Turkey Cabinet move marks a major policy shift after decades of caution over Ankara ties, with decision coming amid worsening Israel-Turkey relations over Gaza war The Israeli government on Sunday unanimously approved recognition of the mass killings of Armenians during World War I as genocide, marking a significant policy shift and a move widely viewed as a rebuke to Turkey. The decision comes as relations between Israel and Turkey have deteriorated sharply following the outbreak of the Gaza war in October 2023, when Hamas launched attacks on Israel. “Historic decision: the Israeli government has unanimously approved Foreign Minister Gideon Saar’s proposal to recognise the Armenian genocide,” Israel’s foreign ministry said in a statement. The decision will still require approval from the Israeli parliament before becoming final. Saar said the recognition was necessary to acknowledge historical facts and criticised what he described as continued efforts to deny or downplay the events. “The Armenian genocide remains to this day the subject of an institutionalised campaign of denial and minimisation, including a manipulative rewriting of history, mainly by the Turkish government,” Saar said during a cabinet meeting, according to a statement from his office. He said Israel, as a Jewish state, had a responsibility to formally recognise the events, calling it both a moral and historical obligation. Successive Israeli governments had avoided formally recognising the Armenian genocide, in part to preserve relations with Turkey, once one of Israel’s closest strategic partners in the region. But since the war in Gaza erupted, Turkey has regularl","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 19:02:39 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/israel-recognises-armenian-genocide-deepening-rift-with-turkey-ws-e-14027111.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"14 killed in Saudi Aramco helicopter crash near Ras Tanura, probe underway","14 killed in Saudi Aramco helicopter crash near Ras Tanura, probe underway","14 killed in Saudi Aramco helicopter crash near Ras Tanura, probe underway At least 14 people were killed after a helicopter operated by Saudi Aramco crashed in Ras Tanura, Saudi Arabia, on Sunday At least 14 people were killed after a helicopter operated by Saudi Aramco crashed in Ras Tanura, Saudi Arabia, on Sunday, the Saudi Energy Ministry said. The accident occurred around 6 AM local time, and all of the people killed were Saudi nationals, the ministry said in a statement. Authorities confirmed that there were no survivors. An investigation has been launched to determine the cause of the crash, though officials have not yet disclosed what led to the accident or released details about the victims. According toReuters, the helicopter went down on Saudi Arabia’s eastern coast, west of the strategically important Strait of Hormuz. Ras Tanura is home to West Asia’s largest oil refinery, which is operated by Saudi Aramco. It is also one of the kingdom’s most important oil export terminals. The crash comes just two days after Saudi Aramco resumed crude oil loading operations at its Ras Tanura terminal following a suspension that had lasted nearly four months due to the Iranian conflict. In a statement carried by the Saudi state news agency, authorities said investigations are being conducted with the participation of the relevant agencies to establish the circumstances surrounding the crash. The ministry didn’t indicate if the incident was in any way connected to a hostile attack, especially from the Iranian side. During the conflict period, Iran had often targeted energy facilities in the Gulf region. According to anAFPreport, the refinery has previously been targeted s","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 18:44:15 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/14-killed-in-saudi-aramco-helicopter-crash-near-ras-tanura-probe-underway-14027110.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"PM Modi calls for equitable climate action, says island nations bear brunt of climate change","PM Modi calls for equitable climate action, says island nations bear brunt of climate change","PM Modi calls for equitable climate action, says island nations bear brunt of climate change Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Sunday said the Global South, especially island nations, was bearing the brunt of climate change, as he underlined the need for equitable climate action in his address to the National Assembly of Seychelles. Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Sunday said the Global South, especially island nations, was bearing the brunt of climate change, as he underlined the need for equitable climate action in his address to the National Assembly of Seychelles. In his speech, he highlighted how the effects of climate change are already visible on coastlines, marine ecosystems, weather patterns and communities. ”The Global South, and especially the island nations, are the most impacted by climate change. Its effects are already visible on our coastlines, in the marine ecosystem, in weather patterns, and in our communities,” said Modi, who is on a three-day visit to the archipelagic nation. Modi also reiterated that countries which had contributed the least to climate change should not bear the greatest burden of its consequences, and stressed that climate action must be guided by “fairness, responsibility and equity”. He further said India and Seychelles shared the vision of a world where development was more inclusive and reaffirmed their commitment to advancing the interests of the Global South. ”This is the spirit that unites the Global South. And this is the vision that India and Seychelles will continue to advance together,” he said. “Seychelles occupies a special place for India’s vision in the Indian Ocean,” the prime minister said. “The Indian Ocean does not separate India and Seychelles. It connects us.” The prime minister also proposed expanding bilateral cooperation in fisheries, marine ","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 17:50:54 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pm-modi-calls-for-equitable-climate-action-says-island-nations-bear-brunt-of-climate-change-14027106.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"France records 1,000 excess deaths linked to Europe's deadly heatwave","France records 1,000 excess deaths linked to Europe's deadly heatwave","France records 1,000 excess deaths linked to Europe's deadly heatwave Europe remained under the grip of an intense heatwave, with temperatures forecast to reach 40 degrees Celsius in several countries even as thunderstorms swept across parts of the continent. Europe remained in the grip of extreme weather on Sunday, with temperatures expected to touch 40 degrees Celsius in several countries even as thunderstorms swept across parts of the continent. France, meanwhile, reported around 1,000 excess deaths linked to the record-breaking heatwave. According to France’s public health agency, most of the heat-related deaths involved elderly people. Officials warned that the toll could rise further as data from care homes and private residences continues to be compiled. Scientists have described the heatwave, which began on June 20, as one of the most severe ever recorded in Europe. The prolonged spell of extreme heat has disrupted power generation, strained healthcare systems and damaged infrastructure across the continent. Researchers also said the intensity of the heatwave would have been “virtually impossible” without human-driven climate change, adding that unusually high night-time temperatures were around 100 times more likely than they would have been two decades ago. Temperatures were forecast to approach or exceed 40 degrees Celsius in parts of Germany, Poland and Italy on Sunday, while storms hit several areas of France, disrupting transport and electricity supplies. In Germany, rail services were reduced on a major route in North Rhine-Westphalia, while tram operations were suspended in the eastern city of Leipzig. Local media reported that many residents chose to remain indoors until temperatures eased after sunset. The extrem","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 17:50:40 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/france-records-1000-excess-deaths-linked-to-europes-deadly-heatwave-14027104.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,11 killed as civilian plane carrying skydivers crashes in northeastern France,11 killed as civilian plane carrying skydivers crashes in northeastern France,"11 killed as civilian plane carrying skydivers crashes in northeastern France At least 11 people were killed after a civilian aircraft crashed in eastern France on Sunday, according to local authorities. At least 11 people were killed after a small civilian aircraft crashed in eastern France on Sunday, news agency AFP reported, citing local authorities. The aircraft came down in the town of Tomblaine, near Nancy in the Meurthe-et-Moselle department, shortly after taking off from the Nancy-Essey airfield. The prefecture said all 11 people on board, including the pilot, died in the crash. French newspaperL’Est Républicainreported that the aircraft was carrying a group of skydivers when it crashed. Local media said there were two groups of parachutists, although authorities have not officially confirmed the details. Emergency services rushed to the site and launched rescue and recovery operations. The local prefecture said authorities were responding to an incident involving a civilian aircraft in Tomblaine. The cause of the crash remains unknown and an investigation has been launched. French Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez is expected to visit the crash site in the coming hours, according to BFMTV. Earlier, the prefecture said the aircraft had taken off from the Nancy-Essey aerodrome before crashing in Tomblaine. “An incident is currently underway in the municipality of Tomblaine involving a civil aircraft that took off from Nancy-Essey aerodrome,” the prefecture said in a post on X. It added that Meurthe-et-Moselle Prefect Yves Seguy had activated the departmental operational centre to coordinate the emergency response, with all operational services involved, and that the pref","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 17:52:42 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/11-killed-as-civilian-plane-crashes-in-france-local-authorities-say-14027093.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Indian-origin businessman returns to Singapore after allegedly leaving 400 migrant workers unpaid,Indian-origin businessman returns to Singapore after allegedly leaving 400 migrant workers unpaid,"Indian-origin businessman returns to Singapore after allegedly leaving 400 migrant workers unpaid Authorities move to recover wages for hundreds of affected workers as firms linked to Ramu Palani Velu face scrutiny over alleged employment violations. An Indian-origin permanent resident has returned to Singapore after allegedly leaving more than 400 migrant workers without salaries for several months, according to a government minister. Ramu Palani Velu, who heads three companies — SK Industries, KPA Engineering and VVR Plant Engineering — had reportedly left workers, mainly from India and Bangladesh, without their wages. The affected employees approached Singapore’s Ministry of Manpower on June 22 seeking assistance to recover their unpaid salaries. Minister of State for Manpower Dinesh Vasu Dash said Velu has since returned to Singapore and that authorities have impounded his passport, Channel News Asia reported. During a visit to a temporary multi-facility accommodation centre where the workers are currently staying, Dash said Singapore would continue enforcing labour laws and take action against employers who violate regulations. He urged workers facing salary issues to approach the Tripartite Alliance for Dispute Management (TADM), which is assisting them in filing claims. TADM is a joint initiative involving the Ministry of Manpower, the National Trades Union Congress and the Singapore National Employers Federation. Dash said community organisations, including the Hindu Endowment and Hindu Advisory Boards and local temples, have also stepped in by providing support packages to help workers manage their daily needs. Earlier, the National Trades Union Congress (NTUC) and the Migrant Workers’ Centre (MWC) announced fina","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 16:44:42 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/indian-origin-businessman-returns-to-singapore-after-allegedly-leaving-400-migrant-workers-unpaid-ws-e-14027076.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"'Instead of pointing fingers, look inwards': India rejects Pakistan's 'baseless' Karachi attack claims","'Instead of pointing fingers, look inwards': India rejects Pakistan's 'baseless' Karachi attack claims","'Instead of pointing fingers, look inwards': India rejects Pakistan's 'baseless' Karachi attack claims The sharp response from the Ministry of External Affairs came a day after Pakistani authorities claimed to have foiled a militant attack on a Sindh Rangers compound in Karachi. India on Sunday firmly rejected Pakistan’s claims linking it to the recent terrorist attack in Karachi, describing the claims as “baseless” and asking Islamabad to focus instead on eliminating the terror infrastructure operating from its own territory. Responding to media queries, Ministry of External Affairs (MEA) spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal said, “We have seen Pakistani reports making baseless allegations against India regarding the recent incident in Karachi. We categorically reject them.” “Instead of pointing fingers at others, Pakistan would do better to look inwards, take credible action against the terror infrastructure on its territory and rid itself of its proclivity to rely on terrorism as an instrument of state policy,” he added. The MEA’s statement came a day after Pakistani security forces said they had thwarted a militant attack on a Sindh Rangers compound in Karachi. Officials said the operation lasted nearly 90 minutes, during which six militants were killed and another was captured alive. The attack took place on Saturday night at the Sindh Rangers’ Bhittai Wing headquarters in Karachi’s Gulistan-e-Jauhar area. According to Pakistani authorities, four Rangers personnel were killed in the assault. Officials identified the attackers as members of Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, a militant faction of the banned Tehreek-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP), which later claimed responsibility for the attack. Pakistani authorities said Special Security Unit (SSU) commandos and the Anti-Terr","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 17:01:06 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/instead-of-pointing-fingers-india-rejects-pakistans-baseless-karachi-attack-allegations-asks-it-to-look-inwards-14027083.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"India, Seychelles unveil 19 outcomes to deepen cooperation in defence, digital payments, space, health","India, Seychelles unveil 19 outcomes to deepen cooperation in defence, digital payments, space, health","India, Seychelles unveil 19 outcomes to deepen cooperation in defence, digital payments, space, health New Delhi’s latest outreach to the Indian Ocean neighbour combines strategic security support with development assistance and technology-driven partnerships. India and Seychelles on Sunday announced 19 key outcomes aimed at widening cooperation between the two countries, covering areas such as defence, maritime security, digital technology, healthcare, education, agriculture and space. The announcements came after talks between Prime Minister Narendra Modi and Seychelles President Patrick Herminie during Modi’s three-day state visit to the Indian Ocean island nation. The latest agreements highlight the expanding partnership between New Delhi and Victoria, with a focus on security cooperation, connectivity, capacity building and development assistance. List of outcomes (19 in total) : State Visit of PM@narendramodito Seychelles ⬇️🇮🇳 🇸🇨pic.twitter.com/XKdgODbIZr According to the Ministry of External Affairs (MEA), the two sides agreed on several major initiatives, including an extradition treaty, cooperation in the peaceful exploration and use of outer space, and measures to introduce UPI-based digital payment systems in Seychelles. The countries also signed an umbrella line of credit agreement with the Export-Import Bank of India and agreed to begin preliminary work for establishing a new Seychelles National Hospital. As part of India’s defence support, New Delhi gifted a fast patrol vessel to Seychelles, handed over 10 utility vehicles and five Laser Radial boats to the Seychelles Defence Force, completed the refit of Coast Guard vessel PS Zoroaster and upgraded a Dornier aircraft with a glass cockpit. India also extended humanita","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 16:40:16 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/india-seychelles-unveil-19-outcomes-to-deepen-cooperation-in-defence-digital-payments-space-health-14027075.html,2026-06-29 02:11:39
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Telangana student found dead in UK; family seeks clarity, early return of mortal remains","Telangana student found dead in UK; family seeks clarity, early return of mortal remains","Telangana student found dead in UK; family seeks clarity, early return of mortal remains A 25-year-old student from Telangana's Kamareddy district was found dead in the United Kingdom under circumstances that remain unclear. The deceased has been identified as S Srinath Reddy. A 25-year-old student from Telangana’s Kamareddy district was found dead in the United Kingdom under circumstances that remain unclear, prompting his family to seek the Centre’s intervention to bring his mortal remains back to India at the earliest. According to aNews18report, the deceased has been identified as S Srinath Reddy. He had moved to the UK around 14 months ago to pursue a Master’s degree at De Montfort University in Leicester. While the cause of his death has not been officially confirmed, his family said they have not received any clear information from the authorities regarding the incident. Speaking to reporters, Srinath’s father, Madhusudan Reddy, appealed to both the Central and Telangana government to assist in the early repatriation of his son’s body. “We still do not know what exactly happened. We request the Central and Telangana governments to help bring our son’s body back home as soon as possible,” he said. According to the family, Srinath last spoke to them on the night of June 22. His father said the conversation was normal, and the student did not appear to be under any stress or emotional distress. Later that evening, Srinath reportedly attended a birthday celebration with friends. A relative, whose son also lives in London, said Srinath’s roommate found him dead the following morning, on June 23. According to the relative, the roommate informed friends that Srinath had allegedly died by suicide. However, the family stressed that they have not rece","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 16:15:55 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/india/telangana-student-found-dead-in-uk-family-seeks-clarity-early-return-of-mortal-remains-14027073.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Rare Celtic prince's grave unearthed in Germany, archaeologists reveal","Rare Celtic prince's grave unearthed in Germany, archaeologists reveal","Rare Celtic prince's grave unearthed in Germany, archaeologists reveal Archaeologists in Germany have uncovered a rare Iron Age princely grave containing gold jewellery, imported artefacts and a wagon, offering new insights into the region's Celtic elite Archaeologists working ahead of the construction of a solar park near Bad Camberg in Germany have uncovered a previously unknown Iron Age grave believed to belong to a high-ranking member of the Celtic elite, with discoveries at the site including gold jewellery, imported artefacts, weapons and evidence of a rare waggon burial. The discovery, announced earlier in June by the Hessian Science and Research, Arts and Culture Ministry (HWWK), was made during preliminary excavations before construction work began. Archaeological teams spent two weeks excavating the grave while ensuring the project remained on schedule. Researchers recovered gold jewellery, imported objects including an Etruscan spouted jug and the remains of weapons from the burial site, leading them to conclude that the grave belonged to a person of considerable status. “With the Early La Tene–period princely grave from Bad Camberg, we can now demonstrate the presence of a local Celtic elite that had previously only been assumed,” said Hesse State Archaeologist Prof. Dr Udo Recker. “The grave, and the opportunity to investigate it using the latest research methods, is of great significance for Iron Age research in Hesse.” X-ray and CT scans carried out on excavated soil blocks have revealed the presence of additional grave goods that are yet to be recovered and analysed. Archaeologists also identified wheel hubs, axle caps and iron tyre fittings, indicating that a two-wheeled waggon had been buried alongside the i","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 15:41:32 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/rare-celtic-princes-grave-unearthed-in-germany-archaeologists-reveal-ws-e-14026990.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,'I don't fear death': Sheikh Hasina confident of returning to Bangladesh this year,'I don't fear death': Sheikh Hasina confident of returning to Bangladesh this year,"'I don't fear death': Sheikh Hasina confident of returning to Bangladesh this year Former Bangladesh Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina has said that she will return to Bangladesh this year despite the death sentence against her, asserting that her political struggle is aimed at restoring democracy Former Bangladesh Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina has said that she will return to Bangladesh this year despite the death sentence against her, asserting that her political struggle is aimed at restoring democracy and protecting the rights of the people rather than seeking power. In an interview withNDTV, Hasina described the verdict against her as politically motivated and maintained that she was not afraid of death. She said she had survived several conspiracies in the past and remained confident that she would once again return to her country. “My return is not a question of personal ambition. It is tied to the restoration of democracy, the rule of law and the political rights of the people of Bangladesh,” she said, adding that she would overcome “every obstacle and every conspiracy” to return home this year. Hasina expressed confidence that the Awami League would regain political ground despite the ban on the party and the cases filed against its leaders and workers. She said the party’s future depended on public support rather than the actions of the government. According to her, the Awami League has repeatedly survived periods of repression during its 77-year history and continues to enjoy grassroots support across Bangladesh. The former prime minister alleged that the current political system lacked democracy and the rule of law, claiming that her party had been kept out of the political process through unconstitutional measures. She urged party workers to remain united a","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 15:36:58 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/i-dont-fear-death-sheikh-hasina-confident-of-returning-to-bangladesh-this-year-14027065.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,PM Modi conferred Seychelles' highest environmental honour: 'Guardian of the Blue Horizon',PM Modi conferred Seychelles' highest environmental honour: 'Guardian of the Blue Horizon',"PM Modi conferred Seychelles' highest environmental honour: 'Guardian of the Blue Horizon' Prime Minister Narendra Modi was on Sunday conferred the 'Guardian of the Blue Horizon', Seychelles' highest honour for leadership in environmental conservation and sustainable development Prime Minister Narendra Modi was on Sunday conferred the ‘Guardian of the Blue Horizon’, Seychelles’ highest honour for leadership in environmental conservation and sustainable development. The prestigious honour was presented during PM Modi’s official visit to the East African island nation. The award recognises his long-standing commitment to sustainable development, environmental protection and international cooperation in addressing global climate challenges. Award of Guardian of the Blue Horizon received by@narendramodiin recognition of his distinguished leadership and commitment to sustainable development, climate resilience and the advancement of shared global objectivespic.twitter.com/AH2hnE8tmo The Presidential Citation also acknowledged PM Modi’s support for Small Island Developing States (SIDS), efforts to strengthen climate resilience and his advocacy for the responsible management of ocean resources. It further highlighted the growing partnership between India and Seychelles in the areas of environmental conservation and sustainable development. Reacting to the honour, PM Modi expressed gratitude to the people and the Government of Seychelles, as well as President Patrick Herminie, for conferring the award upon him. In a post on X, the Prime Minister said that he humbly accepted the honour and dedicated it to all countries that are working to tackle climate change and protect the environment for future generations. Grateful to the people and Government of Seychel","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 15:19:43 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/india/pm-modi-conferred-seychelles-highest-environmental-honour-guardian-of-the-blue-horizon-14027056.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Zelenskyy says Ukraine hits two Russian refineries in overnight drone attacks,Zelenskyy says Ukraine hits two Russian refineries in overnight drone attacks,"Zelenskyy says Ukraine hits two Russian refineries in overnight drone attacks Ukraine launched overnight drone strikes on two Russian oil refineries, with President Volodymyr Zelenskyy saying the attacks were aimed at weakening Russia's ability to wage war Ukraine carried out overnight drone strikes on two Russian oil refineries in the Krasnodar and Yaroslavl regions, President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said on Sunday, as Kyiv stepped up efforts to disrupt Russia’s fuel supply and weaken its war capabilities. Announcing the strikes on social media,Zelenskyysaid Kyiv was continuing operations designed to reduce Russia’s capacity to sustain the war. “We continue our operations that weaken Russia’s ability to wage this war,” Zelenskyy wrote on social media, adding that the refineries were about 300 km and 700 km (190 miles and 430 miles) from Ukrainian territory. Ukraine’s increasingly frequent drone attacks have triggered acute fuel shortages in parts of Russia, one of the world’s biggest oil producers, with queues and rationing reported at petrol stations. Our warriors began Ukraine’s Constitution Day with great accuracy. Last night, our long-range sanctions reached two oil refineries in Russia. The Slavyansk oil refinery in the Krasnodar region was hit – about 300 kilometers from the frontline. We also reached a refinery in the…pic.twitter.com/MiKOSjszFF In a statement posted on Telegram on Sunday, Krasnodar region governor Veniamin Kondratiev said a fire had broken out at the refinery in Slavyansk-na-Kubani. He said one person was killed, while another was injured in a nearby village. Unverified footage circulating on social media showed a large fire at the refinery. The Slavyansk refinery is a privately owned facility with a produc","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 14:24:52 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/zelenskyy-says-ukraine-hits-two-russian-refineries-in-overnight-drone-attacks-14027045.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,World Bank approves $1.4 bn emergency financing for Bangladesh as the country struggles with rising inflation,World Bank approves $1.4 bn emergency financing for Bangladesh as the country struggles with rising inflation,"World Bank approves $1.4 bn emergency financing for Bangladesh as the country struggles with rising inflation The World Bank approved $1.1 billion in emergency financing for Bangladesh to help secure food supplies, support vulnerable households and ​businesses due to the rising prices of fertiliser, fuel ‌and food from the Middle East conflict. The World Bank approved $1.1 billion in emergency financing for Bangladesh to help secure food supplies, support vulnerable households and ​businesses due to the rising prices of fertiliser, fuel ‌and food from the Middle East conflict. Bangladesh is also seeking additional external financing from development partners, including the International Monetary Fund (IMF), to shore up foreign exchange reserves ​and ease pressure on public finances following a surge in ​energy import costs and broader economic challenges. The World Bank ⁠package comprises two projects aimed at helping the country manage ​external shocks and maintain economic stability. Of the total, $300 million will be ​provided under the Emergency Support for Food Security Project to finance imports of 600,000 metric tons of fertiliser for the upcoming rice seasons. Bangladesh imports more ​than 85% of its fertiliser requirements, making it vulnerable to ​disruptions in global supply chains. “Rising food, fertiliser and fuel prices stemming from the ‌Middle ⁠East conflict, coupled with tighter fiscal space, have deeply affected Bangladesh’s economy, particularly smallholder farmers and poor and vulnerable households,” Jean Pesme, the World Bank’s division director for Bangladesh and Bhutan, said in ​a statement. The project ​will support rice ⁠cultivation across 1.4 million hectares (3.46 million acres) of farmland. The remaining $713 million, approved under the Contingent Emergency ​Response Project, will finance","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 14:22:50 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/world-bank-approves-1-4-bn-emergency-financing-for-bangladesh-as-the-country-struggles-with-rising-inflation-14027043.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Rome equips elderly with smart bracelets as Europe battles deadly heatwave,Rome equips elderly with smart bracelets as Europe battles deadly heatwave,"Rome equips elderly with smart bracelets as Europe battles deadly heatwave Rome is using smart bracelets to remotely monitor elderly residents as Europe swelters under an intense heatwave, helping protect vulnerable people living alone As Europe continues to endure a deadly heatwave, Rome is relying on wearable technology to monitor elderly residents living alone, with hundreds of people now using smart bracelets that allow social workers to track their health remotely and respond quickly during emergencies. The initiative, introduced last year by Rome’s municipality using EU post-Covid funding, forms part of a €400-million ($456 million) support programme for older people and currently covers around 700 residents. Among those benefiting from the programme is 85-year-old Roman widow Dina Gazzella, who has lived alone since losing her husband in 2023 and her cat a year later. She now wears an electronic bracelet that monitors her heart rate and sleep patterns while also allowing her to call for help in anemergency. The device, worn like a watch, is fitted with motion sensors that can detect accidental falls and continuously track Gazzella’s movements both inside and outside her home on the eastern outskirts of Rome. “If I feel unwell, this is a lifesaver,” Gazzella told Reuters. Gazzella said the bracelet had given her peace of mind. “They convinced me (to get it) because they told me it was necessary, because I’m alone in the house and if something happens, if I fall, no one will pick me up; instead this one beeps, and someone will come.” Local authorities have described the device as an important preventive health tool astemperaturesin Rome climb into the upper 30s during the ongoing European heatwave. “Th","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 13:37:12 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/rome-equips-elderly-with-smart-bracelets-as-europe-battles-deadly-heatwave-14027032.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"PM Modi holds bilateral talks with Seychelles President Herminie, focus on deepening ties","PM Modi holds bilateral talks with Seychelles President Herminie, focus on deepening ties","PM Modi holds bilateral talks with Seychelles President Herminie, focus on deepening ties Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Sunday held bilateral talks with Seychelles President Dr Patrick Herminie at the State House in Victoria as part of his three-day official visit to the East African island nation Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Sunday held bilateral talks with Seychelles President Dr Patrick Herminie at the State House in Victoria as part of his three-day official visit to the East African island nation. The meeting comes amid efforts by both countries to further strengthen their longstanding partnership across defence, maritime security, development and people-to-people ties. #WATCH| Victoria, Seychelles: Prime Minister Narendra Modi holds a bilateral meeting with Seychelles President Dr Patrick Herminie at the State House.(Source: ANI/DD News)pic.twitter.com/YmpsP1HZE5 Before the bilateral meeting, PM Modi received a ceremonial Guard of Honour at the State House. He was welcomed by President Herminie, following which the two leaders participated in official ceremonial proceedings before beginning their discussions. #WATCH| Victoria, Seychelles: Prime Minister Narendra Modi receives Guard of Honour at State House.PM Modi also meets Seychelles President Dr Patrick Herminie.(Source: ANI/DD News)pic.twitter.com/RYo6HsmN5k PM Modi isvisiting Seychelles from June 27 to 29at the invitation of President Herminie. During the visit, he will also attend the country’s Golden Jubilee National Day celebrations on Monday as the Guest of Honour. Seychelles is marking 50 years of its independence from the United Kingdom, which it achieved in 1976. The meeting between the two leaders is aimed at expanding cooperation in key sectors, including maritime security, defence, capacity buil","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 13:32:37 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/india/pm-modi-holds-bilateral-talks-with-seychelles-president-herminie-focus-on-deepening-ties-14027033.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,King Charles' updated royal role includes protecting 'space for Faith' in multi-faith Britain,King Charles' updated royal role includes protecting 'space for Faith' in multi-faith Britain,"King Charles' updated royal role includes protecting 'space for Faith' in multi-faith Britain Buckingham Palace has updated King Charles III's official role, saying the monarch will protect ""the space for Faith"" in Britain's multi-faith nation. The revised description highlights his commitment to interfaith dialogue while retaining his position as Supreme Governor of the Church of England. Buckingham Palace has published a revised description ofKing Charles III's constitutional role, stating that the monarch, as Supreme Governor of the Church of England, is responsible for protecting ""the space for Faith"" within Britain's ""multi-faith nation."" The updated definition appears in the Royal Family’s annual financial review, the Sovereign Grant Report 2025-26, and expands on the monarch’s responsibilities compared with last year’s version. The previous report described the King simply as the “Head of the Church of England and Defender of the Faith.” The latest edition adds a broader emphasis on religious inclusion, stating: “His Majesty is Supreme Governor of the Church of England and protects the space for Faith within the multi-faith nation.” The revised wording reflects King Charles’ long-standing commitment to interfaith dialogue, a cause he has championed since his time as Prince of Wales. Throughout his public life, the King has regularly engaged with representatives of Jewish, Muslim, Sikh, Orthodox and other religious communities in the UK and overseas, often speaking about cooperation among the Abrahamic faiths. Before his coronation in 2023, there had been speculation that Charles might adopt the title “Defender of Faith” rather than the traditional “Defender of the Faith,” signalling a broader commitment to all religions. However, he ultimately retained the historic title used by previous British monarchs. The report also expands the description of the Ki","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 13:16:29 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/king-charles-updated-royal-role-includes-protecting-space-for-faith-in-multi-faith-britain-14027026.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"While Israel-Lebanon deal stops fighting, legal experts warned Beirut of a bigger issue: Lack of judicial recourse","While Israel-Lebanon deal stops fighting, legal experts warned Beirut of a bigger issue: Lack of judicial recourse","While Israel-Lebanon deal stops fighting, legal experts warned Beirut of a bigger issue: Lack of judicial recourse As Israel and Lebanon sign a ceasefire agreement to stop the fighting, legal experts are warning that the deal could block victims of Israeli war crimes in Lebanon from pursuing accountability and hinder future efforts to give the International Criminal Court jurisdiction in the country. As Israel and Lebanon sign a ceasefire agreement to stop the fighting, legal experts are warning that the deal could block victims of Israeli war crimes in Lebanon from pursuing accountability and hinder future efforts to give the International Criminal Court jurisdiction in the country. On Friday, Lebanon and Israel signed a 14-point framework agreement in Washington, which was designed to work towards an end to fighting between Israel and Hezbollah. However, Article 13 of the deal says that Israel and Lebanon will “cease all hostile or negative actions in international political or legal forums” to establish good faith between the two sides. The broadly framed text could prevent victims of Israeli war crimes allegedly committed during fighting since 8 October 2023 from seeking justice through international or national courts. Legal experts argued the clause would mean that Lebanon would not be able to grant the ICC jurisdiction in the country, which advocates have pushed for to prosecute Israel and its leaders for alleged war crimes. The lawyer argued that the clause would kill any hopes of judicial recourse for the war crimes committed by Israel. “This will kill any hope of granting the ICC jurisdiction, even any hope of a UN fact-finding mission,” said Farouk al-Moghrabi, a former adviser to the Ministry of Human Rights who helped draft a law to give the ICC jurisdiction in Lebanon. He noted that the law would also kill internal efforts to investigate and doccument crimes","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 11:57:22 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/while-israel-lebanon-deal-stops-fighting-legal-experts-warned-beirut-of-a-bigger-issue-lack-of-judicial-recourse-14026958.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,UK heatwave causes supermarket fridge failures as temperatures hit record highs,UK heatwave causes supermarket fridge failures as temperatures hit record highs,"UK heatwave causes supermarket fridge failures as temperatures hit record highs Britain's record-breaking heatwave has caused supermarket refrigeration failures, disrupted transport and public services, and prompted severe weather warnings across the country Britain’s record-breaking heatwave has disrupted supermarkets across the country, with reports of refrigeration failures leaving chilled and frozen food shelves empty as temperatures climbed above 36C in several areas this week. The disruption comes as experts warn that refrigeration equipment is struggling to cope with increasingly frequent periods ofextreme summerheat across the UK. According toThe Grocer, shoppers found empty chiller cabinets at Tesco stores in Haslingden, Crewe and Altrincham after refrigeration systems malfunctioned. The outlet further reported similar issues in Wales, the Forest of Dean, Oxfordshire, Manchester and Birmingham. One resident wrote: “If you’re going to Sainsbury’s to buy chilled or frozen food, don’t bother. They’ve closed off all those sections.” The UK’s hottest June day on record was broken for the third consecutive day on Friday, with the Met Office recording a provisional temperature of 37.3C at Santon Downham in Suffolk. The reading exceeded Thursday’s 36.7C recorded at Merryfield in Somerset, which had itself surpassed the previous day’s 36.1C in Gosport, Hampshire. The new provisional record also eclipsed the previous June record, which had stood for 50 years, by more than one degree. Elsewhere, temperatures reached 35.1C in Hawarden, Flintshire, marking the highest reading in Wales, while Scotland recorded 29.2C in Eskdalemuir, Dumfries and Galloway. Northern Ireland’s highest temperature was 25.6C in Katesbridge, County Down. The","Sun, 28 Jun 2026 11:41:09 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/uk-heatwave-causes-supermarket-fridge-failures-as-temperatures-hit-record-highs-ws-e-14026991.html,2026-06-29 02:11:40
